window.SEARCH_INDEX = [{"c":"Preface","h":"Preface.html","s":"content-intro","t":"Preface","x":"The recent publication of the world report on vision by the World Health Organization in 2019, followed by the publication of the Lancet commission on global health have all brought a renewed focus on eye care. These publications have also highlighted the vast disparities at national level, both in the capacity of eye care services and their availability across geographies within the countries. Also, for the first time, WHO has proposed, visual acuity of < 6/12 as the thresholds for intervention cataract surgery and refractive error. The flipside of all of these is that the eye care infrastructure of eye hospitals and primary eye care centres (Vision Centres) would have to increase multiple times, across the developing countries. Tens of thousands of vision centres and eye hospitals will need to come up to provide easy access and have the capacity to provide eye care to all. While many factors contribute to effective eye care, well-designed physical facility is an important factor as it can bring about efficiency and enhance patient experience. This highlighted a need and an opportunity for high-level guidance and considerations on the design of eye care facilities. This document is an offering towards this."},{"c":"2. Need and scope","h":"chapter1.html","s":"content-intro","t":"The need and scope","x":"In most developing countries, hospital architecture hasn't evolved as a specialized discipline. Hospitals, as all of us are aware, have the specific function of caring for the sick or ensuring well-being. These translate to specific workflows and functional needs in hospitals. For these to happen well, it warrants a deeper understanding of the space requirements, how they relate to each other and what amenities are required in different functional areas. Broad functions in a typical hospital would be, patient intake or registration, outpatient examination including investigations, laboratories, imaging, interventions including surgery and recovery. In addition, there are support functions like housekeeping, dietary, and administration. Teaching hospitals have academic and research functions as well. Each of these functions has space requirements with specific design, features and amenities. Some functions like radiology are governed by mandated specifications to eliminate harm from radiation. Similarly, there are safety codes to minimize risks from fire hazards as well as building codes for hospitals. All of this specialized building knowledge has to be brought in while designing an eye hospital as well. Well-designed hospitals add a lot to enhancing patient experience, efficiency and safety to patients and staff. The guidelines in this manual are meant to provide relevant architectural inputs for those venturing to build an eye care facility and architects working on such projects. As with any such project, it helps to define the boundaries of what this document offers and what it doesn't."},{"c":"2. Need and scope","h":"chapter1.html","s":"content-not-covered","t":"What is not covered","x":"Any building project is governed by space availability, either open land or a building that would be converted into an eye care facility. Such constructions or modifications would also be subject to local rules and regulations. The local architect would be or should be well-versed with such information and regulations. These are varied and specific to each project and its location. Thus, these are outside the scope of this manual. A lot of consideration has to go into the decision to build a hospital, then where to build it, size of land or building required and so on. While these do have a bearing on the building and its design, this manual will not be dwelling on this. This manual builds on from the stage that such a decision has been thoughtfully made and the land or appropriate building for remodelling is decided upon. In any hospital, there are areas that don't have hospital specific requirements, such as offices, other administrative areas, meeting rooms, classrooms, auditorium, common toilets, etc. Hence, these are not covered in this document. Since the hospital size would vary, dictated by factors like patient volumes, this manual doesn't attempt to provide the actual layout for complete outpatient services or operating rooms, with the actual number of examination or operating rooms. However, sample layouts are included."},{"c":"2. Need and scope","h":"chapter1.html","s":"content-covered","t":"What is covered","x":"Several checklists for high-level conceptualization to detailed space planning to ensure that all critical factors and areas considered. Suggested layout of individual rooms for various functions - examination, investigation, surgery, inpatient, etc. Checklists for what all would go into a room as well as other requirements like power, water, data, phone, etc."},{"c":"3. How to use the manual","h":"chapter2.html","s":"content-intro","t":"How to use the manual","x":"This manual is designed to serve as a resource guide while planning for an eye hospital building, by providing practical tips to the clients (refers to ophthalmologists/administrators) and help them to: Scope the building size to reflect the patient volumes and other planned functions. Develop a better understanding of the space requirements and key considerations for optimised space utilisation and layout in an eye care setting. Be adequately prepared to engage architects in the planning and subsequent design phase, which impacts the overall project design and development. In terms of the hospital building requirements, a hospital building consists of three segments - (a) Clinical areas that allow a conducive environment to carry out clinical functions, specific to eye care. Example – outpatient area, operating theatre complex, etc. (b) Patient and staff safety elements that needs to be incorporated. (c) Areas that are common to any hospital or other buildings that follow standard dimensions and design considerations. For example, inpatient rooms, common areas such as the lobby, amenities, toilets, building aesthetics felt as necessary. This manual intends to focus on the first two segments which require domain expertise derived from practical experience, to ensure a patient-centric layout design. The other consideration is the applicability – The overall space requirements of an eye hospital project are determined by patient volumes, the scope of services, aesthetic considerations, and staff strength to manage it. Hence it would not be appropriate to discuss the ideal space requirements of a facility as a whole. On the other hand, what is consistent and applicable to any type of eye hospital are: High-level considerations that impact patient flow and the overall hospital design – eg. clinical work-flow, adjacencies, lighting, orientation, future expansion, support functions and safety considerations for patients and staff. Space considerations at a unit level – for example, dimensions and key considerations of an outpatient consultation room will remain the same irrespective of the hospital size while the number of consultation rooms may vary based on the outpatient volumes and services offered by the facility. Safety considerations for patients and staff utilising the facility. To achieve the above, the manual is divided into four sections as illustrated below. On clinical units largely discusses the design considerations at a functional unit level, though the checklists allow the clients to select the type and number of rooms to arrive at overall space requirements applicable to a specific hospital project. The same is applicable to determine other considerations such as lighting, water sourcing, power load, etc. How to Navigate the Manual The manual can be navigated as per the reader's need, either by approaching it sequentially or go to a specific section or a specific toolkit. Each section has a write-up discussing the key principles and toolkit consisting of checklists, layout illustrations, videos & references. Each toolkit comes with a description and how to use it. Checklists can be viewed online to know the details or downloaded to work out the space requirements applicable to individual settings. However, these checklists are not exhaustive but customizable by including the specific needs as per the local conditions and practices. We would also welcome suggestions for improving it."},{"c":"3. How to use the manual","h":"chapter2.html","s":"content-intro","t":"Safety considerations","p":"How to use the manual","x":"How to use the manual - illustration_00004 image figure illustration","k":"Image","f":"How to use the manual - illustration_00004"},{"c":"3. How to use the manual","h":"chapter2.html","s":"content-intro","t":"How to Navigate the Manual","p":"How to use the manual","x":"How to Navigate the Manual How to use the manual - illustration.pptx image figure illustration","k":"Image","f":"How to use the manual - illustration.pptx"},{"c":"4. Engaging the architect","h":"chapter3.html","s":"content-intro","t":"Engaging the architect and design approval process","x":"A. Introduction The purpose of the design development and approval process is to ensure that the facility is designed to the acceptable and mandated standards, constructed within an acceptable budget and time frame, satisfying the functional, social, and aesthetic expectations of the client (the entity wanting to build the hospital) and patients. The role of the architect is paramount in this design process. Thus, there needs to be mutual clarity both on the design process and on the roles of the client and the architect. While the details are likely to vary from place to place based on the established practices, the following is proposed as a comprehensive template. In reality, the actual responsibility for specific items may shift between the client and the architect."},{"c":"4. Engaging the architect","h":"chapter3.html","s":"content-approval-process","t":"B. The approval process - A five-stage process integrated within the general design process","x":"The approval process consists of the following five stages as illustrated. This design and approval process if done well should ensure the construction drawing; documentation and execution stages can proceed without the need for any intervention from the client team. Each stage is marked with Five-Stage Approval Process This section will focus on stages I, II and IV in detail since the other two stages are straightforward in terms of the details."},{"c":"4. Engaging the architect","h":"chapter3.html","s":"content-approval-process","t":"Five-Stage Approval Process","p":"B. The approval process - A five-stage process integrated within the general design process","x":"Five stages flow image figure illustration","k":"Image","f":"Five stages flow"},{"c":"4. Engaging the architect","h":"chapter3.html","s":"content-preliminary-design","t":"Key stages: 1.Preliminary design submission","x":"a) Purpose To allow the client to identify major design anomalies or misunderstandings of the intent or any statutory/regulatory constraints that may affect the project schedule/outcome before the detailed development of the design. The first submission of the documentation is expected at the schematic design level. Approval in writing to proceed will be a prerequisite for stage II. b) Process Stage I submission will follow: Detailed study of guidelines and the statutory requirements by the architect, a visit to a similar facility, and a request for clarifications if any. The client team will clarify any aspects raised by the architect that may influence the preliminary design. This shall be accomplished within stipulated days of the architect being given these guidelines. The drawings and documents in compliance described on the deliverables for schematic submission shall be submitted in printed and digital form to the client within the prescribed time limit. The client shall evaluate the submission and in writing give comments/suggestions for a revised submission or for proceeding to stage II. If a revised submission is requested, the time for revision shall be agreed upon mutually. The approved documents are then lodged in both hard copy and soft copy (pdf) for documentation purposes by the client. c) Considerations Only two rounds of schematic submissions are recommended to ensure speedy progress. The client and the architect shall work closely to ensure the Preliminary design stage is completed to the satisfaction of the client within the two submissions. d) Deliverables The following drawings and documents shall be submitted in hard and soft copies: Site analysis report, highlighting applicable zoning and building by-laws, levelling and drainage proposals, and environmental considerations. Preliminary master plan showing placement and orientation of blocks, entries and exits, and internal circulation. Schematic floor plans indicating the arrangement of rooms and sections indicating heights. The basement (if applicable) and entry levels regarding the site and roads shall be shown. Any deviation/ non-compliance from the guidelines shall be presented in the form of a deviation document with reasons for the deviation/non-compliance. Statement of areas, coverage, FAR (Floor Area Ratio)/FSI (Floor Space Index). Preliminary design report on explaining the architectural design approach with reference images for understanding form and aesthetics. Any additional drawings/documents which the architect desires. Block estimate."},{"c":"4. Engaging the architect","h":"chapter3.html","s":"content-concept-design","t":"Key stages: 2. Concept design submissions","x":"a) Purpose Concept Design Submission stage II is to enable the client to visualize the project in terms of floor areas, and the number of floors and to gain an understanding of the engineering services and sustainability measures being incorporated. This should enable the client to know how the design meets their vision and requirements. The clinical/management team could begin (a) high-level preparation for equipping and staffing the facility and (b) budgeting for initial recurring expenditures. The client will approve the plans and areas, proposed engineering systems, finishes, and budgetary estimates. Final design submission would enable the client to sign off the design process and allow progression to statutory approvals and construction. b) Process Stage II will commence on written approval to proceed, after the completion of stage I, by the client. The architect shall incorporate any inputs/comments while developing the design. The structural and services engineering inputs will be considered and incorporated. The Master Plan and floor plans with the location of doors and windows and internal dimensions of the rooms will be submitted to show compliance with these guidelines. Any suggestions/comments/inputs will be considered by the architect. Concept design with layouts of furniture will be submitted for the client's approval. Proposals for all engineering systems, finishes, and budgetary estimates submitted will be approved with suggestions/comments by the client to proceed to the final phase of design. The final concept design will incorporate any suggestions/comments/input on the stage II submission and will comprise a master plan with the landscape, floor plans with furniture layouts, envelope design, and interiors. All guidelines in this document, other than expressly accepted deviations would have complied with the design. All statutory requirements are met and provisions for all engineering services are incorporated in the drawings. In essence, every design decision that influences the outcome of the project and requires the consent of the client would be obtained and recorded in drawings and documents and submitted for the approval of the client. c) Considerations If desired, the client may request one revised submission of the master plan and floor plans in stage II to enable finalization and acceptance of the master plan and the floor plans and sign-off on areas. The architect shall incorporate changes in the aesthetics, form of the buildings, and selection of materials as desired by the client. The final designs shall enable the client to sign off inputs on all aspects of the design. d) Deliverables in stage II The following drawings and documents shall be submitted in hard and soft copies: Final master plan with roads, footpaths and drains, and levels. Floor plans with doors and windows and layout of furniture and room inner dimensions and room areas. Architectural design report. Design basis and approach reports for structural, electrical, plumbing, HVAC, fire alarm, firefighting, building management systems, and landscape designs from consultants. Schedule floor wall and ceiling finishes. Statement of areas. Landscape drawing showing hard and soft treatments. Drainage plan. Sections and elevations sufficient to explain the design. Specifications in brief for all materials and systems proposed in the design. Preliminary designs for structural, electrical, plumbing, HVAC, fire alarm, and firefighting and building management systems. Budgetary estimate for the entire project including all engineering services. Artist's renderings of the project, both interior and exterior sufficient for the client to appreciate and approve the design. Scale model of the project (optional)."},{"c":"4. Engaging the architect","h":"chapter3.html","s":"content-statutory-approval","t":"Key stages: 3. statutory approval submission","x":"a) Purpose To obtain a building permit b) Process The architect will identify all the statutory approvals required to commence the construction of the project. The client will be made aware of all the documents required for submission to the statutory authorities. The architect will make available all the technical drawings and documents in the acceptable format with the required signatures and certificates to enable the client to submit applications and obtain approvals. c) Deliverables at stage IV All documents and drawings are prescribed by the various statutory bodies."},{"c":"4. Engaging the architect","h":"chapter3.html","s":"content-flowchart","t":"C. Flowchart","x":"Design & development flowchart Here is the flowchart that illustrates the steps and areas covered in each stage succinctly. This comprehensive diagram shows the complete workflow from initial planning through to final statutory approvals. Click to View Flowchart"},{"c":"4. Engaging the architect","h":"chapter3.html","s":"content-flowchart","t":"Design & development flowchart","p":"C. Flowchart","x":"2 - Building design and development - Flow chart FINAL pdf","k":"PDF","f":"2 - Building design and development - Flow chart FINAL"},{"c":"5. High-level considerations","h":"chapter4.html","s":"content-intro","t":"High-level considerations for building size, layout, and design","x":"While designing an eye hospital, it helps to start with the macro-level or big-picture details and once firmed up, have them direct the micro details. This will ensure that all immediately relevant factors and needs, as well as those that would be in the future, are all considered. The high-level considerations can be grouped into two. One set of considerations would relate to getting right, the adequacy of the building - i.e., building size, and space for all envisaged services or activities. The second set of considerations would help in getting the operational aspects of the building right to promote efficient flows, patient safety, better security, etc. The following details are intended to help in the right building design and future-proof it to some extent."},{"c":"5. High-level considerations","h":"chapter4.html","s":"content-functional-adequacy","t":"A. High-level consideration to ensure functional adequacy of the building","x":"1) Patient volume Patient volume is possibly the most significant influencer of the building size and design. Since buildings cannot expand at will or short notice, it will be good to think about and plan for the ultimate volume that you would like the building to handle (subject to the limitations of space availability). This estimation should be disaggregated for outpatients and surgery. This will then need to be translated into a daily patient load. Simple averages are not realistic, since, in most instances, there tend to be daily and seasonal fluctuations in patient volumes, especially in hospitals that do not have an appointment system. It is advisable to consider all these factors and arrive at the daily patient volume that the hospital should be able to handle in the long run. Example: Estimated annual patient volume: 45,000 OPD visits & 3,000 surgeries Daily average (considering 300 working days): 150 OPD visits & 10 surgeries Daily realistic range: 100 - 250 OPD visits & 5 - 25 surgeries In this scenario, one should ideally plan the building to handle 200 to 250 OPD visits and perform 20-25 surgeries a working day. This will comfortably handle the days with peak patient volumes and accommodate growth to a certain extent. 2) Scope of services - clinical,academic and research All activities eventually will need space, amongst other things. It is in this context, it is important to visualize the scope of services. The following are some of the questions that would need to be thought through: Whether this would be a secondary or a tertiary level facility; if tertiary, the sub-specialities that would be offered; would an eye bank be part of the need? List all support services like different laboratories, radiology, etc.; what would be in-house and what investigations would be referred to external facilities? Would the hospital routinely engage in academic activities; if yes, would it be for internal staff only or others as well? If some are accredited courses, are there mandated space requirements? Clarity on research activities - Some research activities like basic research require dedicated space while clinical trials, retrospective studies and epidemiologic studies would at best need an office space. The size of space needed in both instances would depend on the scale of such activities. Checklist – Scoping the services for layout design Click to view checklist Click to download excel checklist 3) Patient categories - private, free, subsidized & outreach Some providers cater their services to essentially one category of patients. For them, this would not be an important consideration. However, several hospitals are much more inclusive by having people of different economic strata access services in their hospitals. For such hospitals, this would be an important consideration. From a building design perspective, inputs will need to be given on: Patient types and their proportions Patient intake, clinical workflow, and accommodation for each type of patient - what will be the common flow and what will be separate. Service differentiation - while clinical services would follow a common protocol, many providers would want to differentiate the non-clinical domain, like air-conditioning, locating the wards (inpatient stay), restricted visiting hours, dining facilities, etc. 4) Policies Policies have a significant influence on building design and this needs to be recognized upfront. Every provider would have their philosophy and strategy, which in turn would drive their policies governing the operational details. The following have implications from a building design perspective: Is the patient intake through appointments or walk-ins? This, amongst other things, will have a bearing on waiting areas. Will there be inpatients or a fully ambulatory facility with no overnight admissions? Will there be separate areas for private and free/subsidized patients for registration, outpatient examinations, inpatient stay, etc.? z 5) Scope of patient amenities Since patients could be spending several hours in the hospital and most would be required to buy spectacles or medicines, it would be necessary to decide how these would be met. Some important amenities both from patient and building perspectives are: Arrangements for food - cafeteria, coffee shop, etc. Medical shop Optical shop When it comes to optical and medical shops space will be required to hold the inventory and accommodate the back office functions such as accounting, supply chain management and for the opticals, a workspace for surfacing the lenses to the required power and fitting them in the frames. Some aspects of this can be outsourced as well. Planning for this ahead will ensure adequate space allocation and layout to ensure operational efficiency. Patient amenities Click to view video Click to download PPTX 6) Support services Decisions on the support services like cafeteria, laundry, the extent of building maintenance facilities, wastewater treatment, plant for distilled or clean water, centralized UPS (uninterrupted power supply), centralized air-conditioning, hold area hospital waste including discarded furniture and equipment, etc., have a bearing on space requirement and building design. 7) Housing for staff Hospitals need to function round the clock and this is true for eye hospitals too if they would be having inpatients staying overnight. Hence, space permitting, many hospitals would like to have the staff staying on campus. This also eliminates challenges in travel logistics and commute time to work. Good housing also attracts staff to the organization and could help with better retention. This becomes particularly important if renting a good house is a challenge, wherever the hospital is located. Having said all this, there are also pragmatic considerations of the availability of land and finances. For these reasons, many hospitals do not provide on-campus housing for staff. Either way, this input would need to be given at the planning stage. 8) Phasing of the building project Regardless of the financial constraints, it is always a good idea to plan for the full anticipated patient volumes and other activities envisaged. This is on account of the functional dependencies and patient flow requirements. For example, it would be very desirable to have all outpatient clinics and related services (like patient registration, medical records, etc.) in adjoining areas. If these were not planned for in advance, either there would be extensive demolition and remodelling or locating additional outpatient clinics in a different location and this may lead to duplication of some of the services. This would also result in a lot of ongoing non-value-added movement and coordination. This holds good for operating rooms as well, wherein the challenges of managing ORs in multiple locations will be of a higher magnitude. Thus, it is advisable to have a master plan (subject to the limitations of space availability) with the different phases thought through. If financial resources are not available upfront, the building could come up in two or three phases. Ophthalmic technology is evolving very rapidly, and new diagnostic treatment modalities are constantly added. There is also an emerging trend of more and more interventions happening in outpatient clinics like lasers or injections. Even major surgeries done in operating rooms are increasingly ambulatory procedures. With such development constantly happening, it would not be possible to predict the impact of these, on space requirements. Thus, even with detailed planning, hospitals will run into space constraints. Hence to the extent feasible, it is desirable to provide some unassigned areas on each floor. Example: phasing & expansion Click to view video Click to download PPTX 9) Leveraging nature To the extent feasible the orientation of the building, layout of the corridors and rooms can be positioned so as to leverage natural lighting and ventilation to the fullest. This creates a healthier environment and also reduces the lighting and HVAC (Heating Ventilation and Air Conditioning) costs. Example:Leveraging Nature Click to view video Click to download PPTX 10) Environmental considerations Thinking through water treatment or recycling, solar power generation right at the design stage gives more options than when trying to retrofit these. Depending on the number of floors, the rooftop should provide enough area to generate 15% to 25% of the power consumed and this can result in significant recurrent savings, especially with the cost of the solar panels rapidly declining and the grid power cost rising. Housing for staff and leveraging nature as described above, also contribute significantly to reducing the carbon footprint. 11) Maintenance Every part of the building will eventually require maintenance. Some on a daily basis, some periodically and some rarely as the need arises. Maintenance can be expensive and labour-intensive. The quality of maintenance is a strong determinant of the ambience and thus the level of patient and staff satisfaction. Certain types of flooring or wall finishes are easier to maintain than others. Some flooring materials become better with usage (and good maintenance) while others deteriorate with usage, regardless of maintenance. Similarly, some electrical and other fittings (switches, etc.), would literally last a lifetime while some other brands may require frequent replacements. Bulbs in high ceilings are hard to reach and replace. Likewise, external windows or glass cladding for aesthetics will require periodic cleaning and arrangements for this will need to be thought through and provided."},{"c":"5. High-level considerations","h":"chapter4.html","s":"content-functional-adequacy","t":"Checklist – Scoping the services for layout design","p":"A. High-level consideration to ensure functional adequacy of the building","x":"2) Scope of services - clinical,academic and research 2 - Scoping the Services for Layout Design - Checklist pdf excel spreadsheet","k":"PDF · Excel","f":"2 - Scoping the Services for Layout Design  - Checklist"},{"c":"5. High-level considerations","h":"chapter4.html","s":"content-functional-adequacy","t":"Patient amenities","p":"A. High-level consideration to ensure functional adequacy of the building","x":"5) Scope of patient amenities A5 Patient Amenities video powerpoint presentation","k":"Video · PPT","f":"A5 Patient Amenities"},{"c":"5. High-level considerations","h":"chapter4.html","s":"content-functional-adequacy","t":"Example: phasing & expansion","p":"A. High-level consideration to ensure functional adequacy of the building","x":"8) Phasing of the building project A8 Phasing & Expansion video powerpoint presentation","k":"Video · PPT","f":"A8 Phasing & Expansion"},{"c":"5. High-level considerations","h":"chapter4.html","s":"content-functional-adequacy","t":"Example:Leveraging Nature","p":"A. High-level consideration to ensure functional adequacy of the building","x":"9) Leveraging nature A9 Leveraging Nature video powerpoint presentation","k":"Video · PPT","f":"A9 Leveraging Nature"},{"c":"5. High-level considerations","h":"chapter4.html","s":"content-operational-efficiency","t":"B)High-level operational consideration for the building","x":"Several factors, if considered upfront right at the planning stage can make the building more efficient and functional. Some of the more important ones are discussed in this section. 1) People traffic To start with it helps to recognize the functions that are used by many versus by a few. This applies to both patients and staff. For instance, outpatient services tend to be used the most – by both patients and staff who provide the service. Hence, such areas must be easily accessible. So, placing them right at the entrance would make sense. On the contrary, some admin areas like accounts or stores would be accessed by only a few and that too mainly the staff. These could be in the interiors of the building. 2) Restricted access In certain zones like operating rooms, access is restricted to specific staff and patients scheduled for surgery. Cleanliness and sterility must be of the highest order. The people accessing these areas must either know the protocol or must be closely guided (as in the case of patients). Given all this, the layout should not permit unintended people to wander into such areas. Thus, these spaces must be secluded wherein access can be limited and controlled. This would also apply to less critical areas like classrooms, staff meeting rooms, or back-end operations like stores, etc. Example: Flow & building configuration Click to view video Click to download PPTX 3) Dependencies In a hospital, there are a lot of interdependencies. Some require frequent interactions as with laboratories and other common investigations, while some could be occasional as with equipment maintenance departments. If specific supplies (like IOL) must be obtained for each cataract surgery, then it would save a lot of movement and coordination, if there is a store inside or in the proximity of operating rooms. The layout of various services in a building is not easily amenable to changes, later on. If the positioning is not thoughtfully done, it can result in a lot of unnecessary movement, needing coordination and more time to accomplish the tasks. All of this translates to higher staffing needs, criss-cross movement leading to chaos, the potential for errors and compromised patient experience. Wherever there is a high interdependence, especially between specific departments, it would be best to integrate them in the same area - e.g. optometry work-up and ophthalmologist examination, which would be required for every patient, can be designed to be in one integral module. 4) Building orientation and sizing the footprint The footprint of the building or the plinth area is determined by two factors - first by the restriction imposed by the land area and regulations about how much space has to be left around the building and then by the spaces that have to be accommodated in the ground floor. If the land area is not a restrictive factor, then one has the option of deciding the functions that need to be at the entry-level. Priority has to be given to patients. So it will be good to have the patient arrival, registration and general outpatient services at the entry level along with associated support services like reception, patient registration and amenities like a patient cafeteria. It would also help to have some of the administrative departments which have \"external customers\", such as accounts that are frequented by suppliers. Admin departments that predominantly have \"internal customers\" such as the Personnel and HR Department, stores can be at other levels, based on workflow requirements. In general, it is advisable to keep the plinth area to the minimum and build vertically with more floors, without compromising the functional requirements. This would leave land area for future expansion and other needs. Human interactions are also more frequent in smaller buildings/floors. Smaller plinth areas will also have fewer entry/exit points, making it easier from a security perspective. The overall movement also reduces. All these factors can be considered while the high-level configuration of the building. 5) Clinical care protocols and their impact on space requirements and layout Clinical protocols have a direct influence on the space requirements and layout. a) Outpatient care: Overall, there are two clinical pathways followed in outpatient services. In one pathway, the patient enters a room wherein all the investigations and examinations by the ophthalmologist take place. It would have all equipment required for common investigations and examinations. In addition to the ophthalmologist, one or two technicians will move in and out of the room. In the other clinical pathway, the outpatient services are broken down into multiple services, each with its own space, equipment and staff. The separate service stations could be VA measurement, IOP measurement, refraction, etc. In this pathway, the patients move from one station to another while the technicians stay put in the same station. Both are widely adopted and each has its own advantages and disadvantages. From a building design perspective, each requires different space and layout considerations. Thus the mode of examining the patient will need to be communicated to the architect. In addition, the dependencies, as described earlier, will also need to be considered during the outpatient layout planning. One can consider creating a common area for advanced investigations that require expensive equipment and/or special skills, sub-speciality if the projected patient volumes won't yield the required scale for placing them in individual sub-specialty clinics. b) Operating room complex: This tends to be the most complex and also most expensive part of the entire building on account of all requirements that have to be met towards air quality, sterility, positive air pressure, etc. While this will be dealt with in detail in a later section, it is good to recognize that there are some generic flows, commonly practiced. i) Operating room: In many developing countries it is not uncommon to see each surgeon having two operating tables. While the surgeon operates on one, the next patient gets prepped on the second table. This is done to minimize the time between surgeries given the reality of having very few surgeons and often having to perform a high number of surgeries. This is largely to enhance throughput and efficiency. However, in some countries either the regulation of quality standards doesn't permit it, even though there is no evidence associated with an increased risk of infection. In such situations, an operating room has a single table. Even in this instance, higher productivity can be achieved by having a prep area adjoining the operating rooms, so that the patients can be quickly wheeled in. While there are pros and cons with two-table versus single-table systems, it does have an implication on the design of the operating room size and layout. ii) Flows – sterile and unsterile supplies: All operating rooms require sterile surgical instruments, linen and supplies. Likewise the used instruments, linen and waste have to be taken out, some for discarding and the others for cleaning and resterilization. Here the traditional approach was to create a \"dirty corridor\" for the movement of unsterile items and medical waste to their destination within the operating room complex. A recent approach is to recognize that it is the sterile goods that need protection against contamination, so there is the emerging concept of a sterile corridor that connects the CSSD and sterile stores directly to each of the operating rooms through a separate door or a pass box. Both these approaches are in vogue and have different implications for the layout. iii) Flows - patients and staff: In order to ensure utmost sterility inside the operating rooms, it is ideal to create a restricted semi-sterile zone between the general area and the sterile zone leading to the operating rooms. The restricted semi-sterile zone will be accessible only to the patients and OR staff with appropriate preparation and attire. In addition, having a unidirectional flow helps both in maintaining sterility and efficiency. Clearly all of these have to be decided upfront, in view of the design and layout implications. Example: B5b OT complex planning considerations Click to view video Click to download PPTX 6) Building material types for flexibility, realignment, and cost-effectiveness Because of the rapidly advancing technologies that sometimes require changes in clinical protocols, the need to redesign and change the layout occurs periodically. Over the years there has been a sharp drop in length of hospitalization and more and more procedures are becoming ambulatory, making inpatient stay rooms redundant. Need to reorganize, also happens as the patient volume increases. It will be helpful to keep this in mind this long-term possibility during the building design and in the choice of construction materials, without compromising the functionality and patient/staff experience."},{"c":"5. High-level considerations","h":"chapter4.html","s":"content-operational-efficiency","t":"Example: Flow & building configuration","p":"B)High-level operational consideration for the building","x":"2) Restricted access B1 Flow & Building Configuration video powerpoint presentation","k":"Video · PPT","f":"B1 Flow & Building Configuration"},{"c":"5. High-level considerations","h":"chapter4.html","s":"content-operational-efficiency","t":"Example: B5b OT complex planning considerations","p":"B)High-level operational consideration for the building","x":"iii) Flows - patients and staff: B5b OT Complex Planning Considerations video powerpoint presentation","k":"Video · PPT","f":"B5b OT Complex Planning Considerations"},{"c":"5. High-level considerations","h":"chapter4.html","s":"content-resources","t":"List of resources","x":"1. Scoping the services for layout design Comprehensive checklist to help you define and scope all services needed for your eye hospital layout planning. Click to view checklist Click to download excel checklist 2. A5 Patient Amenities Detailed guide on planning patient amenities including cafeterias, medical shops, and optical shops. Click to view video Click to download PPTX 3. A8 Phasing & Expansion Strategic planning guide for phased building projects and future expansion considerations. Click to view video Click to download PPTX 4. A9 Leveraging Nature Best practices for incorporating natural lighting and ventilation in hospital design. Click to view video Click to download PPTX 5. B1 Flow & building configuration Comprehensive guide on patient flow, staff movement, and optimal building configuration strategies. Click to view video Click to download PPTX 6. B5b OT Complex planning considerations Detailed planning guide for operating theater complex including sterile zones, patient flows, and equipment layout. Click to view video Click to download PPTX"},{"c":"5. High-level considerations","h":"chapter4.html","s":"content-resources","t":"1. Scoping the services for layout design","p":"List of resources","x":"1. Scoping the services for layout design 2 - Scoping the Services for Layout Design - Checklist pdf excel spreadsheet","k":"PDF · Excel","f":"2 - Scoping the Services for Layout Design  - Checklist"},{"c":"5. High-level considerations","h":"chapter4.html","s":"content-resources","t":"2. A5 Patient Amenities","p":"List of resources","x":"2. A5 Patient Amenities A5 Patient Amenities video powerpoint presentation","k":"Video · PPT","f":"A5 Patient Amenities"},{"c":"5. High-level considerations","h":"chapter4.html","s":"content-resources","t":"3. A8 Phasing & Expansion","p":"List of resources","x":"3. A8 Phasing & Expansion A8 Phasing & Expansion video powerpoint presentation","k":"Video · PPT","f":"A8 Phasing & Expansion"},{"c":"5. High-level considerations","h":"chapter4.html","s":"content-resources","t":"4. A9 Leveraging Nature","p":"List of resources","x":"4. A9 Leveraging Nature A9 Leveraging Nature video powerpoint presentation","k":"Video · PPT","f":"A9 Leveraging Nature"},{"c":"5. High-level considerations","h":"chapter4.html","s":"content-resources","t":"5. B1 Flow & building configuration","p":"List of resources","x":"5. B1 Flow & building configuration B1 Flow & Building Configuration video powerpoint presentation","k":"Video · PPT","f":"B1 Flow & Building Configuration"},{"c":"5. High-level considerations","h":"chapter4.html","s":"content-resources","t":"6. B5b OT Complex planning considerations","p":"List of resources","x":"6. B5b OT Complex planning considerations B5b OT Complex Planning Considerations video powerpoint presentation","k":"Video · PPT","f":"B5b OT Complex Planning Considerations"},{"c":"6. Outpatient care","h":"chapter6a.html","s":"content-intro","t":"Outpatient care – design considerations and guidelines","x":"Building layout design depends on the available space, service mix and planned volumes. Hence, providing a prescribed layout design for an eye hospital is not feasible. Instead, this architecture guide focuses on preferred practices and considerations for designing an eye hospital. To make it practical, detailing is provided at a unit level. It is hoped that the planners would find this approach useful for the discussions to design the layout. To help with this, three sets of resources are included in this section: High-level layouts (PDFs) - as models to develop a better understanding of the flows, set-up, adjacencies, etc. Layouts at the unit level (PDFs) of consultation rooms, refraction lanes, investigation areas, etc. Detailed checklists – The checklists in the MS-Excel version are designed as tools for the planners to play with. The versions can be downloaded to work out the requirements (space, flows, electrical and waterline layouts, IT networks, etc.). The recent technological advancements in ophthalmic clinical sciences have created an opportunity to provide comprehensive eye care allowing better diagnosis and widening the scope of treatment even for advanced eye conditions in both the anterior and posterior segments. This is further augmented by the integration of artificial intelligence, the Internet of Things (IoT), imaging technologies, etc. All these significantly impact the design and space requirements of eye hospitals with the treatment practices in eye care becoming more ambulatory. Thus the need to allocate a larger space of 50% + of the total built-up area for outpatient services has become a new norm. This section will explore the impact of outpatient flow on space requirements and layout design in eye clinics and provide best practices for optimal space planning and patient flow."},{"c":"6. Outpatient care","h":"chapter6a.html","s":"content-factors-influencing","t":"Section - A","x":"Factors influencing space considerations for an outpatient clinic The range of services and patient volumes will determine the extent of diagnostics that will be made available in an eye hospital and thereby influence space planning. The key factors are discussed in the section below: Topics covered in this section: Service levels of care delivery - Understanding the hierarchy and scope of services Layout design of an outpatient consultation area - Optimizing space utilization and patient flow Investigation & procedure area - Planning for diagnostic and treatment spaces Please select a topic from the sidebar to explore detailed guidelines and considerations."},{"c":"6. Outpatient care","h":"chapter6a.html","s":"content-service-levels","t":"1. Service levels of care delivery","x":"Secondary level: Secondary level focuses primarily on refractive errors and cataract surgery. Diagnostics would include assessment of both ophthalmic and systemic conditions. Space required for diagnostics and investigations in secondary eye care would typically be less than what is required to accommodate doctors consulting rooms, refraction lanes, waiting areas, counselling, etc. A few other practices do exist: Some hospitals may be equipped to carry out comprehensive diagnostics to assess both anterior and posterior segments as well as the medical management (lasers for yag capsulotomy/glaucoma/diabetic retinopathy) Some may run specific clinic days through visiting consultants who may provide surgical services for subspeciality conditions on specific periodicity every month Tertiary Level: In addition to the above, hospitals are equipped and resourced adequately to provide comprehensive care for patients routinely. The space planning for outpatient services is determined by the patient volumes. For example, high-volume settings with significant subspeciality volumes could justify standalone subspecialty clinics. In low-patient volume settings, it would be more pragmatic to have a common investigation area, connected to various subspecialty consulting rooms since they use the same diagnostic equipment (eg. OCT); secondly, they are expensive and third, require an additional number of skilled staff to operate them which would add to the fixed costs especially if the volumes do not justify the additional workforce. The ancillary clinical services such as optical services, pharmacy, biochemistry and microbiology labs, and examination by physicians (to assess systemic fitness) would require additional space if the eye hospitals decide to offer them inhouse – Making these services available under one roof provides a one-stop service for patients resulting in better compliance to treatment and patient experience. Patient flow in a typical outpatient clinic A typical outpatient clinic would consist of the following: Patient registration & cash counters Waiting for areas for different stations Refraction lanes Consultation suites Investigations Outpatient procedure area Counselling (to improve service uptake) Processing patients with insurance where applicable Dispensing outlets such as optical, pharmacy, etc. The general patient flow in an outpatient clinic would be as follows: Patient Flow Diagrams In addition to the above, support services and amenities such as a cafeteria, drinking water points, washrooms, etc. need to be located in the outpatient area. A significant proportion (60% to 70%) of outpatients visiting an eye hospital typically present with eye conditions that may require medication or spectacles and sometimes, Treatment Distribution Chart may not require any intervention at all. The rest of the patients may require additional investigations followed by specific interventions that are carried out either in the outpatient area (lasers or foreign body removals) or in an operation theatre (surgeries, intra-vitreal injections, etc.). In certain regions, more in the west, anti-VEGF injections are administered directly in the outpatient department. Checklist 1 – List of services and investigations Click to view pdf Click to download excel Checklist 2 – Equipment and furniture requirements Click to view pdf Click to download excel"},{"c":"6. Outpatient care","h":"chapter6a.html","s":"content-service-levels","t":"The general patient flow in an outpatient clinic would be as follows:","p":"1. Service levels of care delivery","x":"The general patient flow in an outpatient clinic would be as follows: Picture2 image figure illustration","k":"Image","f":"Picture2"},{"c":"6. Outpatient care","h":"chapter6a.html","s":"content-service-levels","t":"The general patient flow in an outpatient clinic would be as follows:","p":"1. Service levels of care delivery","x":"The general patient flow in an outpatient clinic would be as follows: Picture4 image figure illustration","k":"Image","f":"Picture4"},{"c":"6. Outpatient care","h":"chapter6a.html","s":"content-service-levels","t":"Checklist 1 – List of services and investigations","p":"1. Service levels of care delivery","x":"Checklist 1 – List of services and investigations 1 - OPD - List of Services and Investigations_for HTML pdf excel spreadsheet","k":"PDF · Excel","f":"1 - OPD - List of Services and Investigations_for HTML"},{"c":"6. Outpatient care","h":"chapter6a.html","s":"content-service-levels","t":"Checklist 2 – Equipment and furniture requirements","p":"1. Service levels of care delivery","x":"Checklist 2 – Equipment and furniture requirements 2 - OPD - Checklist for Equipment & Furniture Requirements_for HTML pdf excel spreadsheet","k":"PDF · Excel","f":"2 - OPD - Checklist for Equipment & Furniture Requirements_for HTML"},{"c":"6. Outpatient care","h":"chapter6a.html","s":"content-layout-design","t":"2. Layout design of an outpatient consultation area","x":"The layout of an outpatient clinic is heavily influenced by the workflow protocol followed for the eye exams, which typically includes four key components: (a) Taking history (b) Assessing vision, performing undilated/dilated refraction (c) Assessments such as measuring blood pressure and intraocular pressure, OCT, fundus imaging, the field of vision assessment (d) Consultation with an ophthalmologist Diagnostics for advanced eye conditions will be carried out if suggested by the doctor. Two types of workflow/patient flow are widely practised: (i) Multi-station workflow In low and middle-income countries, the multi-station workflow is followed predominantly which requires patients to move from one station to another while the corresponding staff remains in their designated stations to perform their functions. (ii) Stationary model The stationary model allows for carrying out common assessments and doctors' consultations in fully equipped consultation suites. In this model, patients stay in the same consultation room while the doctors and staff may rotate between exam rooms to perform their clinical duties. In both models, consultation rooms are often connected through service corridors to enable staff to move freely without disrupting patient traffic. Though the overall size of an outpatient clinic may not vary between the two models, there will be noticeable differences in the layout design as well as the patient throughput. These differences can include room sizes and layouts, corridors designed to facilitate staff movement, patient waiting areas, equipment needs, and more. The decision to adopt a particular model is based on several factors, such as the cost, available staffing, and local cultural preferences of the community being served. Layout 1 –Layout design of a consultation room Consultation room 1 Click to view pdf Consultation room – different settings Click to view pdf Consultation room 2 (Paediatric) Click to view pdf Consultation room – 3D Rendition Click to View pdf Layout 2 – Layout design of an outpatient clinic (Multi-station workflow model) Click to view pdf"},{"c":"6. Outpatient care","h":"chapter6a.html","s":"content-layout-design","t":"Consultation room 1","p":"2. Layout design of an outpatient consultation area","x":"Layout 1 –Layout design of a consultation room 1a - Consultation Room pdf","k":"PDF","f":"1a - Consultation Room"},{"c":"6. Outpatient care","h":"chapter6a.html","s":"content-layout-design","t":"Consultation room – different settings","p":"2. Layout design of an outpatient consultation area","x":"Layout 1 –Layout design of a consultation room 1b - Consultation Room - Different Settings pdf","k":"PDF","f":"1b - Consultation Room - Different Settings"},{"c":"6. Outpatient care","h":"chapter6a.html","s":"content-layout-design","t":"Consultation room 2 (Paediatric)","p":"2. Layout design of an outpatient consultation area","x":"Layout 1 –Layout design of a consultation room 2 - Paediatric examination room pdf","k":"PDF","f":"2 - Paediatric examination room"},{"c":"6. Outpatient care","h":"chapter6a.html","s":"content-layout-design","t":"Consultation room – 3D Rendition","p":"2. Layout design of an outpatient consultation area","x":"Layout 1 –Layout design of a consultation room 3d - Consultation Room - 3D Rendition pdf","k":"PDF","f":"3d - Consultation Room - 3D Rendition"},{"c":"6. Outpatient care","h":"chapter6a.html","s":"content-layout-design","t":"Layout 2 – Layout design of an outpatient clinic (Multi-station workflow model)","p":"2. Layout design of an outpatient consultation area","x":"Layout 2 – Layout design of an outpatient clinic (Multi-station workflow model) 3 - OPD - Multi-station workflow of a secondary eye hospital pdf","k":"PDF","f":"3 - OPD - Multi-station workflow of a secondary eye hospital"},{"c":"6. Outpatient care","h":"chapter6a.html","s":"content-investigation-area","t":"3.Investigation & procedure area","x":"Ophthalmic care involves two levels of investigation. (i) Routine procedures The routine procedures that are typically carried out as part of a regular consultation process such as refraction, measuring intraocular pressure and blood pressure for patients aged 40 and above, checking duct patency (applicable South East Asia), and biometry for individuals undergoing cataract surgery. (ii) Advanced diagnostics Advanced diagnostics, such as optical Coherence Tomography (OCT), fundus imaging and Visual field analyzers, are indicated for patients with sub-specialty eye conditions. (iii) Procedure room(s) Procedure room(s) to facilitate clinical procedures, performing laser treatments, a sterile area to administer intra-vitreal injections, foreign body/suture removals, etc. Key design consideration: Placing the diagnostics and procedure rooms as adjacencies to the consultation rooms, along with sufficient waiting areas, is crucial for maintaining a smooth and efficient patient flow. The listed resources below include layout models for the basic investigation room, refraction lane, scan rooms that require dark room settings as well as multi-purpose investigation room. Planners can either view or download the layout models that are provided as PDFs. Layout 3: Refraction lane Click to view pdf Layout 4: Investigation room – basic needs Click to view pdf Layout 5: Investigation room – multi-purpose room This can be used as a refraction room or for other investigations Click to view pdf Layout 6: A typical scan room Click to view pdf"},{"c":"6. Outpatient care","h":"chapter6a.html","s":"content-investigation-area","t":"Layout 3: Refraction lane","p":"3.Investigation & procedure area","x":"Layout 3: Refraction lane 4 - Refraction Unit pdf","k":"PDF","f":"4 - Refraction Unit"},{"c":"6. Outpatient care","h":"chapter6a.html","s":"content-investigation-area","t":"Layout 4: Investigation room – basic needs","p":"3.Investigation & procedure area","x":"Layout 4: Investigation room – basic needs 5 - Investigation Room - Basic Need pdf","k":"PDF","f":"5 - Investigation Room - Basic Need"},{"c":"6. Outpatient care","h":"chapter6a.html","s":"content-investigation-area","t":"Layout 5: Investigation room – multi-purpose room","p":"3.Investigation & procedure area","x":"Layout 5: Investigation room – multi-purpose room 6 - Investigation Room - Comprehensive Module pdf","k":"PDF","f":"6 - Investigation Room - Comprehensive Module"},{"c":"6. Outpatient care","h":"chapter6a.html","s":"content-investigation-area","t":"Layout 6: A typical scan room","p":"3.Investigation & procedure area","x":"Layout 6: A typical scan room 6 - Multipurpose Scan room pdf","k":"PDF","f":"6 - Multipurpose Scan room"},{"c":"6. Outpatient care","h":"chapter6a.html","s":"content-preferred-practices","t":"Section B","x":"Preferred practices in designing an outpatient clinic 1. Standardized room sizes and grid model Adopting standardized room sizes would allow positioning the columns so that they don't come inside the rooms and thus give flexibility in room layout. This design involves constructing rooms with the same length but varying widths. This approach optimizes available space and allows for the placement of load-bearing reinforced concrete pillars without obstructing patient flow or examination areas. The table below provides an example of room dimensions based on this grid model. Grid Model Diagram 2. Construction type To accommodate increasing patient volumes and new clinical and support services, a well-run eye hospital is likely to undergo renovations. Opting for aluminium/plywood partitions or similar materials instead of walled rooms provides greater flexibility to redesign the space when changes are necessary. This approach makes renovations a cost-effective and quicker exercise. Therefore, the type of construction chosen for the hospital should take into consideration the need for future flexibility and adaptability. 3. Standardized workflow protocol A streamlined linear flow of patients throughout the system is enabled by a standardized workflow protocol. This then becomes part of the design consideration. Therefore, it is highly advisable to standardize the clinical processes before consulting an architect. 4. Considerations for each room/space As applicable: i. Power outlets based on source Grid/Generator power: Standard supply for regular electric fixtures Uninterrupted Power Supply (UPS): To protect ophthalmic equipment, computers, and IT networks from voltage fluctuations, UPS is essential, particularly in low-middle-income countries where power supply could be erratic. ii. Water pipelines and drainage Handwashing facilities in the consultation rooms to reduce cross-infections. iii. LAN/Wireless connectivity For patient care management and the use of electronic medical records. iv. Flexible lighting and temperature settings Arrangements to meet the requirements for diagnostics as well as interventions. 5. Patient privacy The design layout should prioritize patient privacy during the consultation process as per the local cultural preferences. 6. Wider doors and corridors Consider designing wider doors at least in a few rooms and corridors to enable unrestricted movement of wheelchairs and stretchers. 7. Wheelchair/stretcher alcove Provision of wheelchair/stretcher alcove in an accessible area. 8. Accessibility for patients with disabilities Although patients with disabilities may be a minority among the outpatients who visit an eye hospital or clinic, it is crucial to provide them with equal access to facilities and services. Therefore, it is important to create disabled-friendly areas that offer wheelchair-accessible toilets, safety railings, and special arrangements for patient movement across different sections of the hospital. These considerations will help ensure that patients with disabilities can experience the same level of care and access as other patients. 9. Anti-skid flooring Anti-skid flooring to reduce chances of a fall (for more details on patient safety, please refer to the section-Patient & staff safety). 10. Provision of utilities Ensure provision of utilities such as toilets for staff and patients, water points, cafeteria, etc. 11. Fire safety It is critical to consider the provision of fire safety (sprinklers, fire extinguishers, smoke detectors etc.) in adherence to local regulations in different locations of OPD. 12. Communication systems A public address system and CCTV network that enables hospital-wide communication/providing instructions is desirable and should be included while planning the electrical network. 13. Multiple cash counters Setting up multiple cash counters can eliminate unnecessary movement of patients/attendants and also reduce the waiting time. This is applicable either in the high volume settings or different services operational on different floors. 14. Separate toilets Planning for separate toilets for staff and patients in the OPD area. This checklist can help the planners to determine the requirements for power supply & wattage, water connections, etc. Since wattage may vary in different countries, this can be discussed with the architect/engineers involved in the construction design and execution. Planners can view the checklist online as a PDF. This can also be downloaded to use it as a planning tool. Checklist 3: Checklist to determine power supply, water connections, etc. Click to view pdf Click to download excel"},{"c":"6. Outpatient care","h":"chapter6a.html","s":"content-preferred-practices","t":"1. Standardized room sizes and grid model","p":"Section B","x":"1. Standardized room sizes and grid model Picture4 image figure illustration","k":"Image","f":"Picture4"},{"c":"6. Outpatient care","h":"chapter6a.html","s":"content-preferred-practices","t":"Checklist 3: Checklist to determine power supply, water connections, etc.","p":"Section B","x":"Checklist 3: Checklist to determine power supply, water connections, etc. 3 - OPD - Equipment & Power Requirements - Checklist_for HTML pdf excel spreadsheet","k":"PDF · Excel","f":"3 - OPD - Equipment & Power Requirements - Checklist_for HTML"},{"c":"6. Outpatient care","h":"chapter6a.html","s":"content-resources","t":"List of resources","x":"I. Checklists 1. Checklist 1 – List of services and investigations Click to view pdf Click to download excel 2. Checklist 2 – Equipment and furniture requirements Click to view pdf Click to download excel 3. Checklist 3 – Checklist to determine power supply, water connections, etc. Click to view pdf Click to download excel II. Layouts 4. Layout 1 – Layout design of a consultation room • Consultation room 1 Click to view pdf • Consultation room – different settings Click to view pdf • Consultation room 2 (Paediatric) Click to View pdf • Consultation room – 3D rendition Click to View pdf 5. Layout 2 – Layout design of an outpatient clinic (Multi-station workflow model) Click to view pdf 6. Layout 3 – Refraction lane Click to view pdf 7. Layout 4 – Investigation room (Basic needs) Click to view pdf 8. Layout 5 – Investigation room (Multi-purpose room) This can be used as a refraction room or for other investigations Click to view pdf 9. Layout 6 – A typical scan room Click to View pdf"},{"c":"6. Outpatient care","h":"chapter6a.html","s":"content-resources","t":"1. Checklist 1 – List of services and investigations","p":"List of resources","x":"1. Checklist 1 – List of services and investigations 1 - OPD - List of Services and Investigations_for HTML pdf excel spreadsheet","k":"PDF · Excel","f":"1 - OPD - List of Services and Investigations_for HTML"},{"c":"6. Outpatient care","h":"chapter6a.html","s":"content-resources","t":"2. Checklist 2 – Equipment and furniture requirements","p":"List of resources","x":"2. Checklist 2 – Equipment and furniture requirements 2 - OPD - Checklist for Equipment & Furniture Requirements_for HTML pdf","k":"PDF","f":"2 - OPD - Checklist for Equipment & Furniture Requirements_for HTML"},{"c":"6. Outpatient care","h":"chapter6a.html","s":"content-resources","t":"2. Checklist 2 – Equipment and furniture requirements","p":"List of resources","x":"2. Checklist 2 – Equipment and furniture requirements 2 - OPD - Equipment and Furniture Requirements_for HTML excel spreadsheet","k":"Excel","f":"2 - OPD - Equipment and Furniture Requirements_for HTML"},{"c":"6. Outpatient care","h":"chapter6a.html","s":"content-resources","t":"3. Checklist 3 – Checklist to determine power supply, water connections, etc.","p":"List of resources","x":"3. Checklist 3 – Checklist to determine power supply, water connections, etc. 3 - OPD - Equipment & Power Requirements - Checklist_for HTML pdf excel spreadsheet","k":"PDF · Excel","f":"3 - OPD - Equipment & Power Requirements - Checklist_for HTML"},{"c":"6. Outpatient care","h":"chapter6a.html","s":"content-resources","t":"• Consultation room 1","p":"List of resources","x":"4. Layout 1 – Layout design of a consultation room 1a - Consultation Room pdf","k":"PDF","f":"1a - Consultation Room"},{"c":"6. Outpatient care","h":"chapter6a.html","s":"content-resources","t":"• Consultation room – different settings","p":"List of resources","x":"4. Layout 1 – Layout design of a consultation room 1b - Consultation Room - Different Settings pdf","k":"PDF","f":"1b - Consultation Room - Different Settings"},{"c":"6. Outpatient care","h":"chapter6a.html","s":"content-resources","t":"• Consultation room 2 (Paediatric)","p":"List of resources","x":"4. Layout 1 – Layout design of a consultation room 2 - Paediatric examination room pdf","k":"PDF","f":"2 - Paediatric examination room"},{"c":"6. Outpatient care","h":"chapter6a.html","s":"content-resources","t":"• Consultation room – 3D rendition","p":"List of resources","x":"4. Layout 1 – Layout design of a consultation room 1d - Consultation Room - 3D Rendition pdf","k":"PDF","f":"1d - Consultation Room - 3D Rendition"},{"c":"6. Outpatient care","h":"chapter6a.html","s":"content-resources","t":"5. Layout 2 – Layout design of an outpatient clinic (Multi-station workflow model)","p":"List of resources","x":"5. Layout 2 – Layout design of an outpatient clinic (Multi-station workflow model) 3 - OPD - Multi-station workflow of a secondary eye hospital pdf","k":"PDF","f":"3 - OPD - Multi-station workflow of a secondary eye hospital"},{"c":"6. Outpatient care","h":"chapter6a.html","s":"content-resources","t":"6. Layout 3 – Refraction lane","p":"List of resources","x":"6. Layout 3 – Refraction lane 4 - Refraction Unit pdf","k":"PDF","f":"4 - Refraction Unit"},{"c":"6. Outpatient care","h":"chapter6a.html","s":"content-resources","t":"7. Layout 4 – Investigation room (Basic needs)","p":"List of resources","x":"7. Layout 4 – Investigation room (Basic needs) 5 - Investigation Room - Basic Need pdf","k":"PDF","f":"5 - Investigation Room - Basic Need"},{"c":"6. Outpatient care","h":"chapter6a.html","s":"content-resources","t":"8. Layout 5 – Investigation room (Multi-purpose room)","p":"List of resources","x":"8. Layout 5 – Investigation room (Multi-purpose room) 6 - Investigation Room - Comprehensive Module pdf","k":"PDF","f":"6 - Investigation Room - Comprehensive Module"},{"c":"6. Outpatient care","h":"chapter6a.html","s":"content-resources","t":"9. Layout 6 – A typical scan room","p":"List of resources","x":"9. Layout 6 – A typical scan room 6 - Multipurpose Scan room pdf","k":"PDF","f":"6 - Multipurpose Scan room"},{"c":"7. Operation theatre complex","h":"chapter6b.html","s":"content-intro","t":"Operation theatre complex – design considerations and guidelines","x":"A. Introduction In the design of an operating theatre complex (OT), it is crucial to ensure the following: A safe and controlled environment for patients undergoing surgical procedures under local/general anaesthesia. Adequate space for perioperative care activities, including post-procedure recovery, particularly for patients who have received general anaesthesia. Efficient workflow and surgical throughput. Maintaining sterility where needed and in the flow of sterile supplies."},{"c":"7. Operation theatre complex","h":"chapter6b.html","s":"content-planning","t":"B) Planning for an Operation Theatre Complex","x":"Construction of an operating theatre complex requires substantial space and a significant financial investment. The actual area required for operating rooms also depends on other essential support areas, such as sterilisation, a sterile supplies room, dress change rooms, patient preparation, and recovery areas. Such support areas are common to multiple operating rooms. Thus, it is prudent to provide the required space in the same area if additional operating rooms are envisaged in the future – this could be either to handle the increased surgical volume or add newer surgical services. Building new operating rooms elsewhere would mean needing extra support spaces, increasing costs and reducing efficiency and space use. To facilitate this, the design must allow for adjoining spaces to be seamlessly integrated into the existing OT complex. Cataracts and some sub-speciality surgeries now require only a short stay after surgery. Hence, the inclusion of the ambulatory ward as an extension to the OT complex enables efficient workflow and better patient care management, including pre-op checks and brief post-op recovery. The number of beds can be planned according to the daily surgical volumes and the patient turnaround time. In recent years, a significant number of patients undergoing eye surgeries also present with systemic conditions and may sometimes develop complications. Planning a mini-ICU area equipped with lifesaving equipment and medicines close to OT and the wards would enable prompt intervention when required. The operating theatre complex should be designed to function as a self-sufficient unit that facilitates patient flow and clinical processes carried out before, during, and after surgical procedures, to support high standards of patient care. The facility is typically divided into (i) a protected zone, (ii) a clean/buffer zone, and (iii) a sterile zone. The layout should facilitate (a) easy movement of patients, staff, and equipment, (b) efficient surgical workflows and optimal patient care by strategically designing the surgical suite's size, location, operating rooms, recovery beds, and support areas based on the estimated daily surgical load."},{"c":"7. Operation theatre complex","h":"chapter6b.html","s":"content-layout-considerations","t":"C) Key Considerations for the Layout Design of the OT Complex","x":"Protected zone Entry/registration/lobby restricted to the patients undergoing surgery Holding and preparation area for patients (separate areas for patient prep, local & general anaesthesia and waiting & recovery area) Staff lounge & admin area Disposal of waste Clean zone/buffer zone Dress change (patients & staff) CSSD (Wash/autoclaving) Sterile stores Sterile zone Scrub area Operating rooms Location of the OT should help reduce contamination and support efficient workflow, taking into consideration space constraints. Ideally, it should be located where the patient/staff traffic is less, therefore avoiding the heavy traffic areas such as outpatient or administrative areas. If the OT complex is situated on the top floor, an additional layer of roofing should be considered to prevent rainwater seepage. Unlike normal ceilings, the ceilings within the OT complex tend to have a lot of equipment and air conditioning fixed to them, which, if not done well, could weaken the structure, leading to leaks. Thus, it is essential to use quality materials and skilled workmanship in constructing the OT complex, especially the ceilings. It is also advisable to avoid toilets right above the operating rooms. Any shortcomings in the quality of construction and materials tend to show up only after a few years. Repairing operating rooms at that time will be a nightmare, and it may (most likely) require suspending surgery for extended periods of time. The location planning should incorporate extended adjacencies such as: Preparation and anaesthesia recovery area Mini-ICU – to manage any medical emergencies that may arise in the operating room or the wards. Ambulatory ward and inpatient wards Patient types: If the hospital serves different patient groups, especially the pay and free, provision for segregated patient flows - entry/exit, registration, and holding areas, can be considered. The design should also incorporate the movement pathway of these patient groups from the ward to OT and back. Alternatively, the surgeries for pay and free can be scheduled at different times or days. This becomes a necessity (a) from a cultural perspective or (b) to ensure exclusivity of services provided to the paying patients, or (c) due to operational reasons such as availability of human resources. Staff amenities: Change rooms and toilets shall be provided for male and female personnel (nurses, doctors, and technicians) working within the operating unit. The change rooms shall have space for donning surgical attire. Staff change rooms can be situated to facilitate unidirectional movement so that personnel entering the change rooms from outside can change and move directly into the sterile zone (scrub area/operating rooms). Resource 1: The resources provided in this section would offer a conceptual framework that comprises an OT complex and beyond. While the actual design may vary, considerations for the critical components mapped with adjacencies in the model layouts can result in a better design that is operationally efficient without compromising on aseptic practices. The planners may either view online or download the layouts (PDFs) and use them as a reference. Some countries may have specific criteria with respect to layout design and flow. It is important that local regulations are factored into the design and balanced with the preferred practices while finalising the layouts. 1. Layout framework for OT complex (inside view): This conceptual framework illustrates different sections, flows and dependencies that influence OT layout design. Click to View PDF 2. OT complex and adjacencies for efficient workflow (extended): Presenting an integrated layout that comprises an OT complex, daycare wards and ICU. i. OT complex with single operating room for a secondary eye hospital: Click to View PDF ii. OT complex with multiple operating rooms for a high-volume eye hospital providing comprehensive eye care services: Click to View PDF Single corridor vs. twin corridor: Layout design plays a critical role in determining how sterile consumables and supplies are transferred from the sterile supplies area to the operating room without compromising aseptic protocols. The \"twin corridor\" design includes a dedicated corridor for the transfer of clean and sterile supplies from the storage area to the operating rooms. Similarly, unclean/used supplies and biomedical waste can be brought to CSSD either through a separate passage or the regular passage used by patients. If the space is not a constraint, it is highly recommended to choose the \"twin corridor\" model. Since this has space implications, it is important to decide at the design stage itself. Design considerations for operating room set-up: The layout design of large tertiary eye hospitals may include separate operating rooms to manage the daily volumes of sub-speciality surgeries thereby reducing the wait times and scheduling challenges. The key difference between these rooms will be the equipment used. In smaller eye hospitals, the same operating rooms may be utilised to perform cataract and other sub-speciality surgeries with proper scheduling. One of the goals of the layout design of an operating theatre complex is to allow an efficient workflow process in addition to keeping a sterile ecosystem. The layout design plays a key role in achieving this – key concepts are listed below: a. Modular design of operating rooms: In recent years, the concept of modular operating rooms has become popular and widely adopted. The key goal is to achieve safe, infection-free, clean and sterile conditions maintained in each operating room. Since modular OTs are built by workers specialising in this, it is more likely to meet the laid-down standards. This will include the following: Separate air handling units (AHU) with HEPA (High-Efficiency Particulate Air) filters and air-conditioning units, along with monitoring and control devices for each operating room. This helps in many ways – for instance, surgical sessions can continue uninterrupted even if one of the operating rooms becomes dysfunctional. This model can also help with better infection control mechanisms, which are paramount to the effective functioning of the OT. Walls and floors Walls: Seamless and impermeable finish to be considered. Intersections of walls should be coved to disallow dust particles from settling in and to help with better cleaning. Operating rooms – PVC cladded, metal with anti-bacterial paint Sterile corridors – PVC cladded, anti-bacterial paint, large format glazed/vitrified tiles Autoclaving and instrument cleaning areas – large format glazed/vitrified tiles Block areas – glazed/vitrified tiles, anti-bacterial paints Ceilings: Smooth and impermeable – e.g., metals such as stainless steel, aluminium, pre-painted galvanised iron sheets. Floors: Smooth, anti-slip, stain-resistant, impermeable flooring materials, such as Epoxy or vinyl, can be considered for operating rooms and corridors in sterile zones. They should be seamless without joints. Colours: Light colours can be considered for the walls, ceilings and floors. Temperature & humidity: Temperature, humidity and air pressure levels should be maintained as per the recommended practices in the operating rooms. Window & split ACs are not recommended for the operating rooms since they are prone to having pockets of microbial growth (as per NABH's revised guidelines, 2018). Positive pressure: The air pressure in the operating room should be maintained at a higher level than ambient air pressure. This will ensure that unfiltered air doesn't enter the operating rooms. Cabling and power sockets: Operating rooms in eye hospitals are equipped with multiple equipment such as operating microscopes, phaco & vitrectomy machines, monitors, etc. It is highly recommended to plan for a ceiling-mount pendant to provide a power supply for the equipment. This will help avoid cables running on the floor and help with the safety of the patients and staff. The pendant should be fixed at a level to accommodate several types of microscopes, since microscopes used for vitreo-retina surgeries and cataract surgeries can vary. To construct modular OTs, one may opt for a prefabricated set-up customised to the specific needs of a hospital or achieve the same by adopting the concept of sourcing the right construction materials. The choice to go with a particular model will be primarily based on the costs, local capabilities, and availability of supplies. b. Efficiency: The capacity utilisation levels of the operating rooms can be enhanced with appropriate workflows when aided by the layout design. From the design perspective, the turnaround time between surgeries can be reduced significantly either by: Adopting a twin table system (while the surgery is in progress on one table, another patient can be prepared on the other table), or Creating a holding area just outside the operating room, where the patient is prepared and wheeled inside. This must be finalised at the design stage since it has direct implications on the space and patient flow process. Some countries may have specific guidelines relating to this, which should be taken into consideration. c. Flow (patients, staff & supplies): Careful attention should be given while planning the workflows, especially the clean and dirty workflows. The layout design can include: Separate pass boxes set up to transfer clean supplies (from the storage area) and used supplies (from OT to the cleaning area) Sterile corridors connecting sterile storage areas with operating rooms if the hospital plans for more than one or two operating rooms. In this case, the used supplies can be taken out through the regular doorway which is used by staff and patients. Autoclaving area for sterile supplies: In an eye hospital, sterile supplies are used by both outpatient clinics, wards, as well as the operating rooms. Hence, it is helpful to provide access to sterile supplies for all groups without compromising the aseptic practices expected of the OT complex. It is recommended to place the autoclaving area & sterile supply storage area within the OT complex. If the available space is limited, it can be planned on another floor with a provision of direct & exclusive access to the operating suite for the passage of sterile and used supplies. This can be achieved either through separate chutes or lifts to transfer clean and used goods. The layout design can incorporate the following: 1) Linen/instruments wash/preparation area provided with access to receiving the dirty linen and used instruments. This section may include the following: A separate room to segregate biomedical waste. Linen washing/preparation (for the linen used only in the OT) – this will include a washing machine and a dryer to wash and dry the linen used in the OT. This is separate from the laundry services to address the needs of the rest of the hospital. Instruments cleaning and packing – this area should include a wash area with water outlets and power sockets to use equipment such as ultrasound cleaners to clean the surgical instruments and a platform to dry and arrange the instrument packs for the autoclaving process. 2) Autoclaving area can consist of separate sections for steam autoclaving and gas autoclaving that uses ethylene oxide (ETO) to sterilize tubings, suctions, etc. Plasma sterilser is another option that can be considered as an alternative since ETO is considered carcinogenic. Steam autoclaves: The currently available steam autoclaves can run both long and short cycles at varying temperatures. Short cycles at higher temperatures can be used to sterilize surgical instruments in between surgeries to allow the teams to perform high volume surgeries in a daily surgical session. The recommended practice is to opt for a horizontal double-door autoclave set-up allowing unidirectional flow. This can be achieved by physically segregating the area into two – (a) for loading the clean but unsterile packs from one end and (b) unloading the packs that completed the autoclave cycle from the other end of the autoclave which opens in the sterile storage area. Depending on the volumes, space can be provided to accommodate an additional horizontal autoclave that can be used to sterilize instruments in between surgeries. This can also help to sterilize an instrument when dropped with no sterile duplicate available to perform surgery. A few hospitals may opt to include ethylene oxide sterilisation (ETO) to sterilize tubing and others that cannot be autoclaved. This may need to be in a well-aerated secluded area that limits exposure & impact of ETO on the staff managing the sterilization process. Note: Short-cycle sterilisation is not suitable for processing cannulated, complex instruments, suction, and other tubing, textiles, paper, or liquids. 3) Storage area: This is an essential part of the OT complex to ensure the availability of sterile supplies for the efficient functioning of the OT. This includes storage areas for: Autoclaved supplies – bins and packs with linen, instruments, and others. Equipment – in most eye hospitals, the same operating rooms will be used for all types of eye surgeries – cataract and advanced surgeries which may require a distinct set of equipment such as Boyles apparatus for general anaesthesia, vitrectomy machine, etc., which may not be used all the time. It may be helpful to store them in a designated storage area when not in use. Similarly, designated bays for resources such as stretchers/trolleys/wheelchairs can be supplied so that these do not obstruct the movement of patients as well as staff. The OT complex layout can include provision of access lanes to pass the sterile supplies to operating rooms as well as to the OPD without disturbing the routine flow of patients and staff in the OT complex."},{"c":"7. Operation theatre complex","h":"chapter6b.html","s":"content-layout-considerations","t":"1. Layout framework for OT complex (inside view):","p":"C) Key Considerations for the Layout Design of the OT Complex","x":"1 - OT Suite - OT Complex Sections and Dependencies pdf","k":"PDF","f":"1 - OT Suite  - OT Complex Sections and Dependencies"},{"c":"7. Operation theatre complex","h":"chapter6b.html","s":"content-layout-considerations","t":"2. OT complex and adjacencies for efficient workflow (extended):","p":"C) Key Considerations for the Layout Design of the OT Complex","x":"2 - OT Flows Single OR Model - Kovilpatti pdf","k":"PDF","f":"2 - OT Flows Single OR Model - Kovilpatti"},{"c":"7. Operation theatre complex","h":"chapter6b.html","s":"content-layout-considerations","t":"2. OT complex and adjacencies for efficient workflow (extended):","p":"C) Key Considerations for the Layout Design of the OT Complex","x":"3 - OT Flows - 3 OT Model - Dindigul pdf","k":"PDF","f":"3 - OT Flows - 3 OT Model - Dindigul"},{"c":"7. Operation theatre complex","h":"chapter6b.html","s":"content-preferred-practices","t":"D) OT Complex Layout Design: Preferred Practices","x":"Natural lighting in the corridors, staff lounge, patient recovery area, CSSD and operating rooms is desirable. Air-tight windows can help achieve this. Scrub areas to have surgeon's sinks fitted with elbow/knee-operated taps set up closer to the operating rooms. Doors to the operating rooms should have doors with sensors for automatic opening and closing, with a viewing window to communicate. Safety and security: Access control for the entry into the OT complex can be considered to eliminate unauthorised entry into the OT complex. As a general security policy, provisions can be provided to set up surveillance cameras at strategic points. Water used in the OT: It is recommended to use filtered water through the reverse osmosis (RO) process. This can prevent any culture growth affecting surgical outcomes. An in-house RO plant with provisions to supply the water to the operating theatre complex can be considered. It is recommended to opt for stainless drums to hold the water at various stages of the filtration process (normal water, RO filter, as well as ultra-filtration process). It is highly suggested to have a separate RO filtering mechanism for the drinking water supply to the staff and patients. Provisions should be given for fire/smoke detection and alarm systems and firefighting equipment (auto-sprinklers, fire extinguishers, etc.) Gas outlets for the supply of oxygen and others should be provided in the pre-operative area where high-risk patients are handled. A designated area (away from the patient care area) to store additional gas cylinders can be considered. A separate bay can be planned, preferably in the semi-sterile/restricted zone, to allow the staff to conduct preventive maintenance and undertake simple repairs that can be managed by the in-house staff trained in instrument maintenance. A mini store inside the OT complex to keep an inventory of sterile consumables (e.g., IOLs, surgical blades, etc.) can help avoid last-minute surprises and improve efficiency. This sub-store can be set up between the dress change area and the operating rooms with easy access for the staff managing the patients in the sterile zone. The area for sub-stores can be compact in size to accommodate a week's consumption of consumables and other surgical supplies. It is recommended to designate an area in the layout design to add additional operating rooms to meet future demand. Checklists These tools will help the planners (a) to make sure essential elements of the OT complex are incorporated and (b) assess the space requirements. Though the checklists are exhaustive, they cannot be considered a conclusive list since technology and requirements keep evolving. The planners should feel free to include added elements as deemed appropriate. Checklist 1 – High-level considerations for OT layout design High-level and detailed checklist for OT complex layout planning. Click to View PDF Click to Download Excel Checklist 2 – Area, procedure and furniture requirements Assessing requirements in terms of specific areas, to perform procedures and furniture for each section of the OT. Click to View PDF Click to Download Excel Checklist 3 – Power and water supply requirements Determining the requirements for power supply for equipment, lighting, etc. and water supply. Click to View PDF Click to Download Excel Checklist 4 – CSSD equipment and wattage Equipment used in the CSSD. Since wattage may vary, a table is provided to document the wattage. Click to View PDF Click to Download Excel"},{"c":"7. Operation theatre complex","h":"chapter6b.html","s":"content-preferred-practices","t":"Checklist 1 – High-level considerations for OT layout design","p":"D) OT Complex Layout Design: Preferred Practices","x":"Checklists 2 - OT Checklist - High Level and Detailed Considerations pdf excel spreadsheet","k":"PDF · Excel","f":"2 - OT Checklist - High Level and Detailed Considerations"},{"c":"7. Operation theatre complex","h":"chapter6b.html","s":"content-preferred-practices","t":"Checklist 2 – Area, procedure and furniture requirements","p":"D) OT Complex Layout Design: Preferred Practices","x":"Checklists 4 - OT - Checklist for Procedures and Furniture Requirements pdf excel spreadsheet","k":"PDF · Excel","f":"4 - OT - Checklist for Procedures and Furniture Requirements"},{"c":"7. Operation theatre complex","h":"chapter6b.html","s":"content-preferred-practices","t":"Checklist 3 – Power and water supply requirements","p":"D) OT Complex Layout Design: Preferred Practices","x":"Checklists 5 - OT - Checklist for Equipment Power and Water Requirements pdf excel spreadsheet","k":"PDF · Excel","f":"5 - OT - Checklist for Equipment Power and Water Requirements"},{"c":"7. Operation theatre complex","h":"chapter6b.html","s":"content-preferred-practices","t":"Checklist 4 – CSSD equipment and wattage","p":"D) OT Complex Layout Design: Preferred Practices","x":"Checklists 7 - OT - Checklist for CSSD Equipment and Wattage pdf excel spreadsheet","k":"PDF · Excel","f":"7 - OT - Checklist for CSSD Equipment and Wattage"},{"c":"7. Operation theatre complex","h":"chapter6b.html","s":"content-resources","t":"List of Resources","x":"The following resources provide additional guidance and reference materials for designing an operation theatre complex. These include layout examples, model layouts and checklists. Resource Downloads 1. Layout framework for OT complex (inside view) This conceptual framework illustrates different sections, flows and dependencies that influence OT layout design. Click to View 2. OT complex with single operating room (secondary eye hospital) An integrated layout comprising an OT complex, daycare wards and ICU, for a secondary eye hospital. Click to View 3. OT complex with multiple operating rooms (high-volume eye hospital) An integrated layout comprising an OT complex, daycare wards and ICU, for a high-volume eye hospital providing comprehensive eye care services. Click to View 4. Checklist 1 – High-level considerations for OT layout design High-level and detailed checklist for OT complex layout planning. Click to View Click to Download 5. Checklist 2 – Area, procedure and furniture requirements Assessing requirements in terms of specific areas, procedures to perform, and furniture for each section of the OT. Click to View Click to Download 6. Checklist 3 – Power and water supply requirements Determining the requirements for power supply (equipment, lighting, etc.) and water supply. Click to View Click to Download 7. Checklist 4 – CSSD equipment and wattage Equipment used in the CSSD; since wattage may vary, a table is provided to document the wattage. Click to View Click to Download"},{"c":"7. Operation theatre complex","h":"chapter6b.html","s":"content-resources","t":"1. Layout framework for OT complex (inside view)","p":"List of Resources","x":"1. Layout framework for OT complex (inside view) 1 - OT Suite - OT Complex Sections and Dependencies pdf","k":"PDF","f":"1 - OT Suite  - OT Complex Sections and Dependencies"},{"c":"7. Operation theatre complex","h":"chapter6b.html","s":"content-resources","t":"2. OT complex with single operating room (secondary eye hospital)","p":"List of Resources","x":"2. OT complex with single operating room (secondary eye hospital) 2 - OT Flows Single OR Model - Kovilpatti pdf","k":"PDF","f":"2 - OT Flows Single OR Model - Kovilpatti"},{"c":"7. Operation theatre complex","h":"chapter6b.html","s":"content-resources","t":"3. OT complex with multiple operating rooms (high-volume eye hospital)","p":"List of Resources","x":"3. OT complex with multiple operating rooms (high-volume eye hospital) 3 - OT Flows - 3 OT Model - Dindigul pdf","k":"PDF","f":"3 - OT Flows - 3 OT Model - Dindigul"},{"c":"7. Operation theatre complex","h":"chapter6b.html","s":"content-resources","t":"4. Checklist 1 – High-level considerations for OT layout design","p":"List of Resources","x":"4. Checklist 1 – High-level considerations for OT layout design 2 - OT Checklist - High Level and Detailed Considerations pdf excel spreadsheet","k":"PDF · Excel","f":"2 - OT Checklist - High Level and Detailed Considerations"},{"c":"7. Operation theatre complex","h":"chapter6b.html","s":"content-resources","t":"5. Checklist 2 – Area, procedure and furniture requirements","p":"List of Resources","x":"5. Checklist 2 – Area, procedure and furniture requirements 4 - OT - Checklist for Procedures and Furniture Requirements pdf excel spreadsheet","k":"PDF · Excel","f":"4 - OT - Checklist for Procedures and Furniture Requirements"},{"c":"7. Operation theatre complex","h":"chapter6b.html","s":"content-resources","t":"6. Checklist 3 – Power and water supply requirements","p":"List of Resources","x":"6. Checklist 3 – Power and water supply requirements 5 - OT - Checklist for Equipment Power and Water Requirements pdf excel spreadsheet","k":"PDF · Excel","f":"5 - OT - Checklist for Equipment Power and Water Requirements"},{"c":"7. Operation theatre complex","h":"chapter6b.html","s":"content-resources","t":"7. Checklist 4 – CSSD equipment and wattage","p":"List of Resources","x":"7. Checklist 4 – CSSD equipment and wattage 7 - OT - Checklist for CSSD Equipment and Wattage pdf excel spreadsheet","k":"PDF · Excel","f":"7 - OT - Checklist for CSSD Equipment and Wattage"},{"c":"8. Ambulatory and inpatient care","h":"chapter6c.html","s":"content-intro","t":"Ambulatory and inpatient care – design considerations","x":"Introduction Ambulatory and Inpatient Care In ophthalmic care, patients undergoing surgery, or a procedure aren't normally required to stay in the hospital. However, in some instances a short stay or a multi-day stay may be necessitated by the level of clinical observations required during the post-operative recovery process. Sometimes patients who travel long distances may opt for a multi-day stay to complete the cycle of undergoing clinical procedure/surgery and the immediate post-operative follow-up. The ambulatory/inpatient care consists of the admission process, stay in the wards and discharge from the hospital. The key functions include – Admission/discharge counter(s) Ward – short and multi-day stay Patient admission process which includes cross-checking the paperwork for each patient. Managing patient records (electronic/ paper) Accommodating patients admitted to the hospital after cross-checking the paperwork Managing billing and financial transactions Extending clinical care and supervision by the doctors and nurses as per the defined protocol during the patient's stay. Allocation of beds (for extended stay) Clinical exams and diagnostics pre-operatively and post-operatively Patient discharge process Handling patient transfers to another health facility when such a need arises (due to medical emergencies) Preparing the discharge summary and related paperwork to discharge patients Since ophthalmic surgeries and procedures are largely elective, admissions can be planned for both ambulatory and multi-day stays depending on the surgery scheduling and patients' convenience. The emergency admissions which are infrequent, can be handled as per the situation."},{"c":"8. Ambulatory and inpatient care","h":"chapter6c.html","s":"content-intro","t":"Introduction","p":"Ambulatory and inpatient care – design considerations","x":"Introduction Picture1 image figure illustration","k":"Image","f":"Picture1"},{"c":"8. Ambulatory and inpatient care","h":"chapter6c.html","s":"content-design-considerations","t":"8. Ambulatory and inpatient care","x":"A. Ambulatory/inpatient care unit: design considerations a)Admission/discharge counter area Can be planned as a separate lane along side the outpatient registration. This is to ensure an efficient work process, HR, handling of financial transactions and at the same time optimize available space. A separate area and counters for the admission process can be considered in a high-volume setting that handles 100+ admissions a day. This is to avoid bottlenecks and delays in outpatient registration. These counters should be provided with adequate storage area for general stationery supplies and medical records of patients who will be undergoing surgery/procedure on a given day. This planning will avoid any delays due to the retrieval of medical records. This wouldn't be a consideration for hospitals fully on electronic medical records. Other considerations include: Waiting area for patients to avoid crowding at the counter Locating it close to public amenities such as toilets for patients, drinking water Television displays for communication & awareness creation Alcove to hold wheelchairs, trolleys, etc. Safety provisions for staff and secured storage system for money handling b) Ambulatory care ward Most patients increasingly opt for short-stay in an eye hospital. Some hospitals, by design, offer only ambulatory without any overnight stay. The number of beds in an ambulatory area is dependent on the daily expected surgical load. It is preferable to plan for curtains between the beds to maintain patient privacy. A small reception area can be planned to receive the patients and verify that all pre-op work-up has been carried out as per the protocol. Post-op counselling is critical to ensure better cooperation by the patients. A small area equipped with an audio-visual system with adequate space can be planned to facilitate group or individual instructions for post-op care and follow-up. This area ideally can be part of the operating room can be located to allow easy access to the operating rooms. c) In-patient unit (multi-day stay) A couple of options can be considered when planning for the multi-day stay. Inpatient ward within the hospital building The eye hospitals with outreach screening initiatives may house the patients for a couple of nights since they may be brought from a distance and cannot afford to stay outside. Hence it is ideal to include general wards with a nursing station and a doctor's exam room for immediate post-op exams. If the hospital handles low volumes, these patients can be examined in the regular OPD at a specific time of the day. Based on the volume of paying patients, where they come from and the type of surgeries done, it would be appropriate to include a few private rooms (suite/single/twin sharing). Private rooms can be of the size standard hotel room (10 ft x 20 ft including bath/toilet). Hospitals that plan to admit patients for overnight stay, it is important to understand that such stays are now not due to medical reasons but more for patient convenience and hence more akin to hotel stay. This perspective opens up other options for providing stay facilities to patients. Some of the hospitals in US and UK have successfully experimented with the following options: Option A: Constructing a building with a hotel-type stay with basic amenities ensures a clean environment. This can either be run by the hospital or outsourced to a reliable partner. Option B: Where possible, tie up with hospitality service providers located closer to the hospital premise to accommodate patients undergoing an eye surgery. This reduces capex costs and eliminates operational expenditure. This approach can reduce the need to recruit a workforce to manage inpatient care, especially the caregiving team and to some extent, the support team as well."},{"c":"8. Ambulatory and inpatient care","h":"chapter6c.html","s":"content-safety-considerations","t":"8. Ambulatory and inpatient care","x":"B. Key Considerations to ensure the safe stay of patients a) Fire exit To provide an alternate exit in the case of emergencies as per the local building code b) Natural lighting Design to allow natural lighting in the ward areas c) Flooring Durable, non-slippery flooring to avoid falls d) Open areas If possible, include open areas with free seating arrangements for patients and the attendants to relax during their stay e) CCTV systems Provisions for CCTV systems to monitor routine functions and for security reasons f) Public address system Public address system to communicate information or instructions at the time of emergencies g) Emergency call facilities Emergency call facilities for staff and patients in all the areas accessed by patients such as patient toilets and bathrooms"},{"c":"8. Ambulatory and inpatient care","h":"chapter6c.html","s":"content-resources","t":"8. Ambulatory and inpatient care","x":"List of Resources 1. Inpatient care checklist Applicable for the scenario where the hospital plans to construct an inpatient unit within the hospital premise Click to download excel 2. Clinical exam room in the ward area Design specifications and layout for clinical exam room within ward area Click to view pdf"},{"c":"8. Ambulatory and inpatient care","h":"chapter6c.html","s":"content-resources","t":"1. Inpatient care checklist","p":"8. Ambulatory and inpatient care","x":"1. Inpatient care checklist A - Inpatient Care Checklist - Rooms- Equipment - Furniture excel spreadsheet","k":"Excel","f":"A - Inpatient Care Checklist - Rooms- Equipment - Furniture"},{"c":"8. Ambulatory and inpatient care","h":"chapter6c.html","s":"content-resources","t":"2. Clinical exam room in the ward area","p":"8. Ambulatory and inpatient care","x":"2. Clinical exam room in the ward area 1 - Wards - Clinical exam room pdf","k":"PDF","f":"1 - Wards - Clinical exam room"},{"c":"9. Patient and staff safety","h":"chapter7.html","s":"content-intro","t":"Patient and staff safety considerations in the layout design of eye hospitals","x":"A. Introduction Though we don't immediately connect hospital buildings to patient and staff safety, in reality, buildings have a significant impact, both on safety and efficiency. This section brings out that connection explicitly and offers insights on how patient and staff safety can be factored in, both while planning for new hospitals as well as how existing building spaces can be made safer. People visit hospitals to get well. However, the potential for falls, accidents, cross infection and exposure to hazards are high in hospitals due to the inherent complexity of hospital buildings, the high number of first-time visitors, their age, anxiety, disabilities, and unfamiliar equipment and procedures. Good building design that considers these would maximise physical safety and give psychological reassurance to patients and comfort and ease of working for hospital staff. Unlike equipment or staffing, buildings can't be easily altered or enlarged for a multitude of reasons, ranging from feasibility to regulatory challenges. Thus, it is important that right at the planning stage we consider two high-level factors – anticipated patient volumes at full capacity and workflow based on the clinical protocols. This consideration applies to outpatient visits, surgeries and other treatment procedures."},{"c":"9. Patient and staff safety","h":"chapter7.html","s":"content-safety-building-design","t":"B. Safety considerations in building design","x":"Hospital buildings tend to be custom-built to accommodate required clinical procedures as well as the promoter's preferences. Patients who are already vulnerable, often find the hospital experience intimidating, especially during the initial visits, due to unfamiliarity with the process and flow. Multiple entries (Emergency, OP, IP) with signage or lack of it, disorienting patients, is just one example. An understanding of the workflow requirements, appreciation of patient needs and factoring in anticipated growth of patient volumes, will all help in designing an efficient and safe hospital. Such understanding can also help make necessary changes in existing facilities, to enhance patient safety (ref: 2). In all this we need to recognize two important factors – first, by design in an eye hospital most patients tend to be visually challenged and elderly and second, some patients are likely to be the source of cross infections, due to other illnesses (ref: 1). The following are some of the more important considerations. 1. Building configuration and workflow The layout of various clinical and patient-related administrative services should match the workflow. The space allocation for each of the services should reflect the patient volumes. When this isn't done, it results in bottlenecks, increased travel distances and criss-cross movement of patients and staff. The greater the movement, the more the scope for falls and accidents, thereby resulting in lower efficiency. 2. Planning for growth in patient load and changes in technology As a hospital's reputation grows, so does the number of patients as well as the staff and equipment. New equipment and procedures would also require additional space. Under these circumstances, additional spaces for outpatient services and operating rooms must be contiguous to existing ones. This would ensure that the building layout continues to mirror the workflow (ref: 3). This requires some foresight matched by appropriate provisioning in the plans. In the absence of this, it results in ad-hoc expansion which often duplicates equipment, staffing and a lot more movement. It must also be appreciated that insufficient and improper space allocation will result in overcrowding and compromised safety and caregiving processes."},{"c":"9. Patient and staff safety","h":"chapter7.html","s":"content-safety-routine-work","t":"C. Safety considerations in routine work (ref: 4)","x":"The safety considerations can be broadly grouped into two, as in everyday work and rare, but with catastrophic consequences. While some of these may be mandated by regulations, it is in the best interest to address them proactively. The following are lists of safety measures, further grouped under broad safety concerns. 1.0 Everyday safety This relates to potential safety issues likely to occur every day as part of the routine caregiving process. There is a tendency in all of us to get used to challenges and shortcomings and figure out ways to work around them, rather than fixing them. So, it is important to do periodic audits, preferably by external persons. The following could serve as a guide or checklist, for existing or new buildings being planned. 1.1 Preventing falls from heights, slips, and trips Guard rails and barriers of sufficient height and strength in both interior and exterior spaces should be provided. Interior and exterior floor surfaces should have sufficient traction to prevent slips and falls. Clear visibility with good lighting at ramps, stairs and corridors. Avoid sudden changes in floor levels at unexpected locations. Wherever water is used, ensure it drains readily and doesn't create slippery surfaces. 1.2 Preventing collisions All clear glass partitions should have visual barrier strips or designs to prevent anyone from mistakenly trying to walk through. Use swing doors rather than sliding doors. People in a hurry tend to assume doors swing open and walk into sliding doors. It is also easier to operate swing doors while pushing wheelchairs and trolleys. Use appropriate handles to open doors – a handle if a door has to be pulled open and a horizontal bar when a door has to be pushed open. Where two directional movements are expected, use double swing doors with view panels. The speed and force of auto-close doors should be calibrated to avoid accidents. Avoid projections and low-hanging sign boards. Avoid doors opening into corridors, ramps and stairs. 1.3 Prevention of workplace issues Task lighting should be considered. Avoid glare on computer screens and worktops from bright lights and windows. Adjustable blinds can help in controlling glare from windows. Good lighting ensures that labels and reports are properly read and reduces the chances of errors. A noise-free environment enhances concentration and productivity. Communication between patient and caregiver is clearer, less ambiguous and more effective in spaces with low ambient noise. Plan the internal layout of the room – position furniture, equipment and cables to enhance ease of work and safety. Position equipment and power outlets appropriately. At all costs avoid cables snaking across floors or hanging in the air. All hazardous equipment should be housed and used as recommended, with proper safety warnings posted. Appropriate safety gear should be given to staff working with potentially hazardous equipment like lasers. Good washrooms and break areas away from patients are often missed but essential. 1.4 Adequate ventilation and good indoor air quality Maximize natural and good cross ventilation. Courtyards bring natural ventilation and lighting into deep hospital buildings. Courtyards also act as social and spillover spaces, a great reference point for navigation and seamlessly connecting the outdoors with the indoors. Courtyards can expand capacities during emergencies as well. Where natural ventilation is not feasible, plan on mechanical ventilation as per standards and work area requirements. Hospital air conditioning needs are very specific and may not be met by domestic units. Consult experts for proper air conditioning. It's also important to have optimal relative humidity. Use materials and furnishings that are low emitters of indoor air contaminants and volatile organic compounds (VOCs). 1.5 Electrical safety All electrical installations should strictly be done as per the respective country's electrical safety codes. Good earthing increases the safety as well as the life of sensitive electrical equipment. Integrate safety devices in the electrical system to prevent short circuits and overloads. Label all electrical panels. Have circuit diagrams displayed in panels for quick reference during emergencies. Use colour coding of switches and power outlets to identify UPS power, power with standby and power without standby. If wetness is noticed in and around an electrical installation, get it attended to on priority. Replace immediately any exposed or frayed cables. Do not plug in multiple pieces of equipment on the same outlet. 1.6 Hazardous materials and waste handling Hospital processes may use and/or generate hazardous materials. Identify possible hazardous materials that need to be handled and lay out clear guidelines (SOPs) to isolate, remove and manage such materials. Separate holding/storage areas must be planned for hazardous materials away from public use spaces. Waste generated in hospitals, including contaminated materials and sharps, requires careful handling. Source segregation helps in proper management of waste and recycling. Design locations for collection bins in all areas of waste generation that ensure easy deposition and safe removal. Disposal of contaminated waste should abide by local regulations, and in their absence, by practices advocated by WHO. 2.0 Rare occurrences Rare occurrences, as the name implies, may never happen — but when they do, the impact is often catastrophic. So, it is all the more important that there is adequate preparation and periodic mock drills and checks to ensure the readiness or fitness of the precautionary measures in place. This should be done even when not mandated. The following is a listing of potential rare occurrences and the required preparedness. 2.1 Fire protection Modern hospital buildings use electro-mechanical systems and fossil fuels which increase the possibility of fire hazards. Fire protection engineers must be involved in all aspects of design to ensure the protection of human life and to reduce potential losses to equipment and property from fire. The building and fire codes must be adhered to. Exit paths should be identified and kept clear of any obstruction at all times. Areas of refuge – a place to safely gather to be rescued by fire personnel – in multi-storey buildings should be designated and maintained. Proper fire detection, alarm and suppression systems can save life and property. Emergency lighting and power systems should function and be kept in readiness at all times through regular checks and maintenance. It is important to have fire-related information and signage displayed in local languages. Regular fire drills and training of staff should be given priority. 2.2 Earthquakes Most national building codes specify the highest degree of earthquake resistance for hospital buildings. Hospitals not only have to be safe for their patients and staff but also should be able to function fully to provide relief to all affected in the area in the aftermath of earthquakes. The integrity of the building to withstand earthquakes needs to be taken care of by experienced structural engineers and proper construction. All large and heavy equipment should be properly anchored at all times to ensure that they do not cause damage during earthquakes. Emergency preparedness for earthquakes should be part of the safety protocol of hospitals. 2.3 Storms, heavy rain, snow, flooding, landslides, etc. Many natural and seasonal occurrences could create safety issues for hospitals. Good site selection – higher ground, away from flowing water bodies, well-drained – helps to a great degree in mitigating possible safety issues during such natural calamities. One may not have the luxury of an ideal site. Good engineering design can then greatly reduce damage from such hazards. All equipment and systems meant to work during such eventualities, like storm drains and pumps, should be in working condition at all times and located in areas unlikely to be affected by flooding, etc. 2.4 Security from human threats Security risks could range from pilferage and theft to vandalism and terrorist attacks. Hospitals are open to everyone and this makes them vulnerable to threats. However, the number of entrances could be planned, balancing functional needs, efficiency and security concerns. Surveillance systems should be planned without compromising privacy. The hospital design should be such that patients' and visitors' movement can be contained based on needs, and passing through unnecessary areas is avoided. Areas of minimal movement like parking garages and storage areas should be closely monitored."},{"c":"9. Patient and staff safety","h":"chapter7.html","s":"content-good-building-design","t":"D. Good building design","x":"Safety considerations in hospital buildings can go hand-in-hand to create a friendly environment and an enjoyable experience. Courtyards, skylights, corridors leading towards points of visual interest/views, designed niches for parking stretchers and equipment and recesses, and the use of colour and texture serve functional needs and enhance the spatial experience. Modular planning and standardisation could bring easy comprehension of a hospital layout and provide flexibility, with minimal physical alteration. Great hospital designs are people-centric."},{"c":"9. Patient and staff safety","h":"chapter7.html","s":"content-references","t":"E. References","x":"Ulrich RS, Zimring C, Zhu X, DuBose J, Seo HB, Choi YS, et al. A review of the research literature on evidence-based healthcare design. HERD 2008;1(3):61-125. Reiling JG, Knutzen BL, Wallen TK, McCullough S, Miller R, Chernos S. Enhancing the traditional hospital design process: a focus on patient safety. Jt Comm J Qual Saf 2004;30(3):115-24. Health Facility Briefing System. International Health Facility Guidelines: Part B: health facility briefing. Version 5, October 2016. healthfacilityguidelines.com (accessed 21 January 2021). Joseph A, Rashid M. The architecture of safety: hospital design. Curr Opin Crit Care 2007;13(6):714-9."},{"c":"9. Patient and staff safety","h":"chapter7.html","s":"content-references","t":"Ref 1 - A Review of the Research Literature on Evidence Ba","p":"E. References","x":"Ref 1 - A Review of the Research Literature on Evidence Ba pdf","k":"PDF","f":"Ref 1 - A Review of the Research Literature on Evidence Ba"},{"c":"9. Patient and staff safety","h":"chapter7.html","s":"content-references","t":"Ref 2 - Enhancing the Traditional Hospital Design - Focus on Patient Safety","p":"E. References","x":"Ref 2 - Enhancing the Traditional Hospital Design - Focus on Patient Safety pdf","k":"PDF","f":"Ref 2 - Enhancing the Traditional Hospital Design - Focus on Patient Safety"},{"c":"9. Patient and staff safety","h":"chapter7.html","s":"content-references","t":"Ref 3 - iHFG part b outpatients unit","p":"E. References","x":"Ref 3 - iHFG_part_b_outpatients_unit pdf","k":"PDF","f":"Ref 3 - iHFG_part_b_outpatients_unit"},{"c":"9. Patient and staff safety","h":"chapter7.html","s":"content-references","t":"Ref 4 - Joseph2007","p":"E. References","x":"Ref 4 - Joseph2007 pdf","k":"PDF","f":"Ref 4 - Joseph2007"},{"c":"9. Patient and staff safety","h":"chapter7.html","s":"content-resources","t":"List of Resources","x":"Resource Downloads Ref 1 - A Review of the Research Literature on Evidence Ba Click to view pdf Ref 2 - Enhancing the Traditional Hospital Design - Focus on Patient Safety Click to view pdf Ref 3 - iHFG_part_b_outpatients_unit Click to view pdf Ref 4 - Joseph2007 Click to view pdf"},{"c":"9. Patient and staff safety","h":"chapter7.html","s":"content-resources","t":"Ref 1 - A Review of the Research Literature on Evidence Ba","p":"List of Resources","x":"Ref 1 - A Review of the Research Literature on Evidence Ba Ref 1 - A Review of the Research Literature on Evidence Ba pdf","k":"PDF","f":"Ref 1 - A Review of the Research Literature on Evidence Ba"},{"c":"9. Patient and staff safety","h":"chapter7.html","s":"content-resources","t":"Ref 2 - Enhancing the Traditional Hospital Design - Focus on Patient Safety","p":"List of Resources","x":"Ref 2 - Enhancing the Traditional Hospital Design - Focus on Patient Safety Ref 2 - Enhancing the Traditional Hospital Design - Focus on Patient Safety pdf","k":"PDF","f":"Ref 2 - Enhancing the Traditional Hospital Design - Focus on Patient Safety"},{"c":"9. Patient and staff safety","h":"chapter7.html","s":"content-resources","t":"Ref 3 - iHFG_part_b_outpatients_unit","p":"List of Resources","x":"Ref 3 - iHFG_part_b_outpatients_unit Ref 3 - iHFG_part_b_outpatients_unit pdf","k":"PDF","f":"Ref 3 - iHFG_part_b_outpatients_unit"},{"c":"9. Patient and staff safety","h":"chapter7.html","s":"content-resources","t":"Ref 4 - Joseph2007","p":"List of Resources","x":"Ref 4 - Joseph2007 Ref 4 - Joseph2007 pdf","k":"PDF","f":"Ref 4 - Joseph2007"},{"c":"10. Energy efficient sustainable hospital infrastructure","h":"chapter8.html","s":"content-intro","t":"10. Championing Responsible Healthcare by Creating Energy Efficient Sustainable Hospital Infrastructure","x":"Contributed by Smart Joules The global healthcare sector's climate footprint is equivalent to 4.4 percent of global emissions, contributing around 2 gigatonnes of carbon dioxide into the environment. This is bigger than the carbon footprint of other industries like aviation and shipping, and equivalent to 514 coal-fired power plants. Reports also say that if healthcare were a country, it would be the fifth largest emitter on Earth. At the same time, with 0.6 beds per 1,000 population against a global average of 2.9 and 4.3 in China, India's healthcare sector is poised to grow rapidly. It is therefore imperative to practice Efficiency First while designing, building and operating hospitals to meet India's burning need. The good news is that simple and proven methods to make substantial reductions in carbon impact exist today, and that most sustainable options are also the most economical. 10 Practical Tips While Planning Infrastructure Development Here are the 10 tips across procurement methods, design practices, equipment selection, O&M practices and culture, distilled from a decade-long experience of reducing carbon impact by 29% with above-market returns on investment across 49 large Indian hospitals ."},{"c":"10. Energy efficient sustainable hospital infrastructure","h":"chapter8.html","s":"content-procurement","t":"1. Procurement","x":"a. Procurement move to performance contracting models Since individual institutions are unlikely to be subject matter experts in energy, one of the most potent tools at leadership's disposal is to move towards performance contracting related to their main energy-consuming systems. For instance, if one insists on buying cooling at a fixed price per ton-hour rather than separately buying design services, equipment such as chillers, pumps and cooling towers, and O&M services to run the infrastructure, the industry would naturally gravitate towards optimising energy efficiency to minimize cooling cost. b. Choose lowest lifecycle, not capital costs Start asking your procurement teams to present comparative analysis of various capital equipment in terms of total cost of ownership over its useful life, including capital costs, maintenance costs and resource (energy, water, chemicals) costs. Often you will find that the lowest lifecycle cost equipment is also the most sustainable option, since the largest component of lifecycle cost of ownership is not the equipment itself, but the cost of energy it consumes over time."},{"c":"10. Energy efficient sustainable hospital infrastructure","h":"chapter8.html","s":"content-design","t":"2. Design","x":"a. Adopt adaptive thermal comfort The single largest energy consumer in any hospital is the cooling system. Cooling system designers must apply the concept of adaptive thermal comfort, which recognises that facilities needn't maintain fixed temperature conditions throughout the year since people's preferences change with seasonal changes and external weather conditions. It allows facilities to achieve higher energy efficiency and more comfortable indoor climates for occupants, while enhancing chances for top green building ratings and boosting overall facility value. b. Choose passive and water-based cooling Passive cooling technologies and practices harness natural processes to maintain comfortable indoor temperatures. If practised at the design stage, they can minimise reliance on mechanical cooling systems, achieving a lower carbon footprint, lower up-front and operational costs, enhanced thermal comfort, greater space availability, and higher facility value. Passive cooling includes natural ventilation, shading and sun control techniques, using materials with high thermal mass (e.g., concrete, brick) that absorb and store heat during the day and release it at night in the building structure, interior walls and floors, cool/green roofs, evaporative cooling, structure cooling, optimal building orientation, etc. If there is still a need for mechanical cooling to cover remaining loads or for dehumidification, one should rely on water-based systems (water-cooled chillers, radiant cooling) vs. air-based ones (air-cooled chillers, VRVs, split ACs) since they offer 40-50% higher energy efficiency levels and much lower lifecycle costs of ownership. c. Choose electric vs. solid, liquid or gaseous fuels Hospitals often rely on solid (coal), liquid (furnace oil, diesel, LPG) and gaseous (PNG, CNG) fossil fuels to fulfil their heating requirements for kitchens, laundries, sterilization departments and domestic hot water needs (bathing). Electrification of heating by using heat pumps and electric boilers offers a profitable decarbonization alternative, especially since the carbon intensity of electricity overall is rapidly declining with the widespread adoption of renewable energy."},{"c":"10. Energy efficient sustainable hospital infrastructure","h":"chapter8.html","s":"content-equipment","t":"3. Equipment","x":"a. Ask for premium efficiency models Designers, OEMs and contractors routinely choose lower efficiency models as their default choice since they are more mainstream and often slightly cheaper in capital cost. However, high efficiency models exist for almost any appliance or equipment that one needs to install in a hospital, including lighting (LEDs), fans (BLDC fans), motors (IE5 motors), cooling towers (low approach cooling towers), refrigerators (5-star rated), etc. Asking partners to opt for the highest efficiency models by default will lead to more sustainable and, over the long term, more economical and reliable infrastructure. b. Choose variable speed equipment Hospital needs are dynamic in nature. For instance, the need for cooling, hot water and steam changes within a day and across seasons with changes in occupancy and weather. Therefore, the equipment and systems that serve them need the ability to also calibrate their output to maintain high efficiency levels under various loading scenarios. This necessitates the need to aggressively opt for variable speed drives (VSDs / VFDs) in all the large (>1.5 kW) blowers (AHUs), pumps (raw water, STP, HVAC) and compressors (chillers, air compressors) installed in our hospitals. VFDs typically have very short payback periods (<1 year) since the ratio of reduction of power input to reduction of system output follows a cubic law — a 30% reduction in air flow saves 65% of power from an AHU."},{"c":"10. Energy efficient sustainable hospital infrastructure","h":"chapter8.html","s":"content-operations","t":"4. Operations and Maintenance","x":"a. Utilize 100% of STP water on-site Most hospitals spend massive amounts of money on meeting their water needs through fresh water from municipal connections, tankers or borewell systems, while at the same time discharging inadequately treated water to their surroundings. Properly designed and well-operated sewage treatment plants can allow hospitals to reuse close to 100% of their wastewater on-site in applications such as cooling, flushing and gardening. This leads to minimal fresh water costs as well as better alignment with existing and future regulations around wastewater disposal. Companies such as Digital Paani, Boson White Water and ECOSTP provide comprehensive solutions for hospitals' needs. b. Practice continuous monitoring and reporting against relevant KPIs The key to successful implementation of any decarbonization or energy efficiency drive lies in formulating a set of compelling key performance indicators (KPIs) and instituting systems to continuously monitor and report against these KPIs. For hospitals, these key metrics include kWh/bed, kW/ton for cooling, running hours of key energy-guzzling equipment, set points and area temperatures maintained, kWh consumed every month by the top 10 energy consuming equipment, etc. Infrastructure to measure, record, store, display, analyse and report such data is extremely cost effective today and must be opted for by default by hospitals."},{"c":"10. Energy efficient sustainable hospital infrastructure","h":"chapter8.html","s":"content-culture","t":"5. Fostering a Culture of Sustainable Practices","x":"Moving sustainability from the basement to the boardroom Perhaps the most important initiative of all is to culturally practice sustainability, starting with leadership teams. If leadership starts asking the right questions to the right people at the right time, results will follow. For instance, sustainability metrics around energy and water are reported every month by site engineering teams to unit heads, by unit heads to corporate leadership teams, and then in turn to the Board of Directors in companies such as KIMS and Aster. Other chains such as Apollo encourage their Chief Engineers to apply for national and international conferences and competitions around energy efficiency and sustainability, offering them a chance to enhance their knowledge and aggression levels with respect to sustainability. Smart Joules is an Indian climate-tech startup founded in 2014 that helps large commercial buildings and hospitals reduce their energy waste and cooling costs. It uses IoT and artificial intelligence systems to optimise energy use through a pay-as-you-save model."}];